}
// ── the type-name DEPTH WALK: the checker's `tyTopIndexOf` IS the home ────────
// EIGHT functions in this file hand-wrote the identical bracket ladder — `{`/`[`/`(`
// open, `}`/`]`/`)` close, `<` open, and a `>` that closes only an OPEN `<`
// (`tyGtIsClose`) — and differed ONLY in what they do when the depth is 0. The rules
// are one thing, and this program has already paid for their being eight: the `<>`
// repairs #1118 / #1120 / D-CLASSANG shipped had to be applied to each copy
// separately, one PR at a time, because a copy that had not been visited yet ran its
// depth NEGATIVE on a generic argument list. #1146 folded the eight into two local
// homes (`tyTopLevelIndexOf` "FIND" + `tyTopLevelSplit` "SPLIT"); THIS slice folds the
// FIND form into the checker's `tyTopIndexOf`, one module down, and deletes it — its
// three call sites (`nullablePartOf`, `annArrowAt`, `nameIsStructWithUnionField`) now
// spell the home directly rather than paying a frame to permute two arguments.
//
// AND THAT IS A BUG FIX, NOT TIDYING. The emitter's ladder was quote-BLIND where the
// checker's has skipped quoted literal members (escape-aware, via `skipQuotedName`)
// since #1143 — so a separator INSIDE a literal member (`Box<"a,b" | "c" | null>`) read
// as top-level here and the annotation was rebuilt from the rubble. Graded over a
// 15-text x 10-position x {plain, nullable} matrix, the two ladders disagreed on a
// population of 24 cells spanning NINE characters (`, { } [ ] ( ) <` and `=>`) and six
// positions; this merge closes 15, and `emit_classify`'s private `shapeInnerFieldSplit`
// — the last quote-blind copy in the chain — owns the other 9. Two of the 24 were not
// even loud: they emitted a module the runtime could not parse. See
// `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md`.
//
// The home's `'='` means the function ARROW `=>`, not a bare `=`: it matches only when
// the NEXT character is `>`. That two-character lookahead is the ONE thing `annArrowAt`'s
// copy of this ladder ever added, and it now lives at the home, not in a private walk
// that has to be re-taught every grammar repair. `sep` must not itself be a grouper
// (every caller separates on `|` or `,`): the grouper arms are tested first, exactly as
// each copy tested them. The home returns at the FIRST hit — `nullablePartOf`'s original
// copy scanned to the END of the name and kept only the first bar, so this is the same
// answer for strictly less work.

// THE LOCAL HOME of the type-name depth walk, "SPLIT" form: append the TOP-LEVEL
// (grouper depth 0) `sep`-separated parts of `s` to `out`.
//
// `dropEmpty` is the ONE axis its four callers differed on: `gaeSplitArgs` drops an
// empty part (a tolerated trailing comma in a generic argument list), while
// `annSplitParams` / `annSplitPipe` / `shapeInnerFieldSplit` keep it — an EMPTY input
// yields exactly ONE empty part, which each of those reads as its "no parts /
// malformed" case (`annFnDecompose`'s empty-param guard exists because of it).
//
// Parts are VERBATIM SLICES of `s`. Two of the four copies accumulated the part one
// code point at a time (`cur = cur + fromCodePoint(c)`), the copy-on-concat shape
// `splitUnionAtoms` was already repaired for; slicing makes the home strictly cheaper
// than the copies it replaces, not merely equal.
//
// It is a RESUME LOOP over the checker's `tyTopIndexOf` rather than its own ladder, for
// the same reason (and with the same quote fix) as the FIND form the section header above
// describes. Resuming the scan at `end + 1` costs ONE pass over `s`, not one per part: the home
// counts depth FROM `from`, and a resume point is always a position where the depth was
// 0 by the split's own condition, so restarting the count there reproduces it. That
// property is the home's, documented at its definition, and it is what keeps this off
// the O(n^2) path.
export function tyTopLevelSplit(
  s: string,
  sep: i32,
  dropEmpty: boolean,
  out: string[],
) {
  let start = 0
  let going = true
  while going {
    let end = tyTopIndexOf(s, sep, 0, start)
    if end < 0 {
      end = s.length
      going = false
    }
    const part = s.slice(start, end)
    if part.length > 0 || !dropEmpty { out.push(part) }
    start = end + 1
  }
  0
}
// THE ONE HOME of the type-name depth walk, "WRAPS" form: does the group opened by
// `name[0]` run to the END of `name` — i.e. does NO closer bring the depth back to 0
// before the final character?
//
// This is the THIRD form of the same ladder, and it was hand-written FOUR times across
// the compiler with THREE different ladders and TWO different readings of the same
// question — a spread that is itself the argument for one home:
//
//   * `emit_collect.nameIsSingleShape` — the FULL ladder (`{}` `[]` `()` `<>`), quote-
//     BLIND, over a `{…}` group. Retired into this home, and the FUNCTION itself is now
//     gone too: once `emit_classify.nameIsWholeSpanShape` stopped hand-walking its own
//     braces (D-SPANHOME) the two bodies were character-identical, so one name survives.
//   * `emit_base.normTypeAtom` — a PAREN-ONLY ladder over a `(…)` group. Retired into
//     this home; its header claimed the paren-only reading was load-bearing ("a `>` in a
//     `$fnsig` key or a `tyToStr` render must not be able to unbalance it") and that is
//     already false of THIS ladder: `tyGtIsClose` makes a `>` with no open `<` inert, so
//     `ii>i` and `->` cannot unbalance it either. Probe P2 measures the two ladders apart.
//   * `emit_classify.monoUnwrapParens` — a paren-only ladder, one module UP, and the LAST
//     hand-written paren-only wrap walk in the compiler. **GONE (D-MONOPAREN).** #1150
//     moved its five out-of-file consumers here and filed the remaining twelve as a
//     measured hand-off; the twelve now call this home directly and the walk is deleted.
//     Measured at BOTH heads rather than inherited: #1150 read 456,003 invocations / 0
//     disagreements over all 17 sites, and the re-measurement at the deletion's own head
//     reads **1,112,807 invocations (31,005 corpus + 1,081,802 fuzz) / 0 disagreements**,
//     with the inverted comparator firing on 100% of reaches on both channels and at every
//     one of the twelve sites individually. Site 7 has ZERO fuzz reach and 2 corpus
//     reaches — the corpus is the only channel that covers it, which is why a fuzz-only
//     verdict would have been a coverage claim, not an agreement claim.
//   * `typecheck.parenEnclosesWhole` — the checker's `tyGroupEndIndex(name, 1) ==
//     name.length - 1`, which is a DIFFERENT reading, not just a different ladder: on a
//     group that NEVER closes (`{{x}`, `(((a)`) it answers FALSE where this home answers
//     TRUE (as did all three emitter copies before they retired into it).
//     `peelGroupParens` rides the checker's and deliberately keeps riding it — the two
//     readings are named here so no later slice merges them by eyeball. NB the two
//     surviving entry points now BOTH gate the never-closes case away before calling:
//     `nameIsWholeSpanShape` requires a final `}` and `normTypeAtom` a final `)`, and both
//     populations are measured empty (`P_WSNC` 0 of 199,621; 0 of 19,680 at #1150).
//
// Callers gate on the OPENER themselves (`name[0] == '{'`, `t[0] == '('`) exactly as the
// copies did, so the scan starts at index 1 with the opener already counted.
//
// THE WALK IS GONE, AND THE DIVERGENCE THAT KEPT IT IS IN THE COMPARISON, NOT THE HOME.
// `tyGroupEndIndex` IS this ladder — same openers, same `tyGtIsClose` gate for `>`, same
// "stop at the first closer that would take the depth below 0" — and it additionally
// skips a QUOTED literal member, escape-aware, via `skipQuotedName`. The one thing that
// kept a hand-written copy here was the never-closes reading, and `parenEnclosesWhole`
// spells that away by comparing straight to `name.length - 1` (so `-1` reads as FALSE).
// The home itself does NOT throw it away: it returns an INDEX and reserves `-1` for
// "never closes", so the emitter's reading is one `e < 0` arm on top and this function
// does not need a loop at all.
//
// #1156 declined exactly this merge — "on a group that never closes it answers TRUE
// where the checker's reading answers FALSE; merging it is a behaviour change needing
// its own graded population, not a cleanup". The behaviour that decline protected is
// preserved EXACTLY by the `e < 0` arm; what it read as one indivisible reading is two
// independent things, a LADDER and a COMPARISON, and only the ladder was ever shared.
//
// The only behaviour that does change is the quote skip, and that is the fix rather than
// a cost: a quoted CLOSER inside a literal member (`{v: "a}b" | "zz"}`) closed this group
// EARLY, so `nameIsSingleShape` answered FALSE for a name that IS a single inline shape.
// Measured over the corpus and a 360-cell graded matrix, the two readings differ on 7
// live invocations (1 of 1,352 corpus, 6 of 71 matrix) and NO channel observes one —
// see the blind-spot table in `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md`. Reached and
// inconsequential is not the same claim as unreached, and only the second would license
// leaving it wrong.
//
// This was `emit_base`'s LAST hand-written type-name walk, and the `tyGtIsClose` call it
// contained was the file's last off-list scanner call — the file now holds zero of both.
export function tyGroupWrapsWhole(name: string) {
  const e = tyGroupEndIndex(name, 1)
  if e < 0 { return true }
  e == name.length - 1
}
// ── nullable struct refs (`S | null`) ─────────────────────────────────────────
// "S|null" / "null|S" (exactly TWO `|` parts, one of them `null`) → the non-null
// part's name, or "" when the name is not a two-part nullable.
export function nullablePartOf(name: string) {
  // Depth-aware: a `|` INSIDE an inline shape (`{y:string|i32}|null`) is part of
  // the FIELD's union, not the nullable wrapper — only a depth-0 bar splits. And a `|`
  // inside a quoted literal member is part of the member's TEXT: the home skips it.
  const bar = tyTopIndexOf(name, '|', 0, 0)
  if bar < 0 { return "" }
  const a = name.slice(0, bar)
  const b = name.slice(bar + 1, name.length)
  if a == "null" { return b }
  if b == "null" { return a }
  ""
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is a NULLABLE STRUCT (`S | null` with a
// resolvable struct part), else `0`. Feeds the `kind == 9` (nullable struct ref,
// `0x63`) family.
// True if the annotation node at `tyIx` is `boolean|null` — the NICHE scalar
// nullable (host: `nullSentinel`): an i32 holding 0/1, with the spare value 2
// encoding `null` (no box). `== null` is a sentinel compare; narrowing to
// non-null is purely type-level (the i32 is already 0/1 in the branch).
export function tyIsNulBool(tyIx: i32) {
  if tyIx < 0 { return false }
  // STRUCTURAL first (destringify): where the checker recorded a type for the node, the
  // ARENA decides — a `TyNullable` over the `boolean` prim (`nodeTyIsNulBool`), the dual of
  // the `nullablePartOf(name) == "boolean"` surgery below. Ladder-faithful (D1 leg C): an
  // UNCOVERED node keeps the annotation-name path unchanged. Mirrors `retNulStringFlag`,
  // whose `nodeTyIsNulString` leg is this test's `string` sibling.
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 { return nodeTyIsNulBool(tyIx) }
  const ty = P.nodes[tyIx]
  // `nameIsNulBool` IS `nullablePartOf(name) == "boolean"` — this arm hand-wrote its
  // body eight lines above the definition, so the niche's spelling had two homes.
  if ty is TypeRef { return nameIsNulBool(ty.tyName) }
  false
}
// True if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `boolean`/`bool`.
export function tyIsBool(tyIx: i32) {
  if tyIx < 0 { return false }
  const ty = P.nodes[tyIx]
  if ty is TypeRef { return ty.tyName == "boolean" }
  false
}
// True if `name` is a nullable i32-BACKED list — `i32[] | null`, `boolean[] | null`, or a
// literal-union `K[] | null` / `("a"|"b")[] | null` (parser-joined `…|null`, depth-aware either
// side of the bar). `boolean[]` (an i32 0/1) and a litunion array (interned-i32 atom elements)
// share the i32-list REP exactly, so they ride the SAME nullable i32-list kind/field-code (18 /
// `nullist`) as `i32[] | null` — one predicate, every i32-backed leaf, mirroring `nameIsI32Array`
// at the non-nullable position. A scalar-elem (`f64[]|null`, `i64[]|null`, `f32[]|null`,
// `string[]|null`) or ref-elem (`S[]|null`) list has a DISTINCT backing and keeps failing loudly.
export function nameIsNulI32List(name: string) {
  nameIsI32Array(nullablePartOf(name))
}
// A `string | null` field type name — the niche FIELD (code 20): a `(ref null $aTypeIdx)`
// storage slot (the array backing a string), the field analogue of the kind-16 local.
export function nameIsNulString(name: string) {
  nullablePartOf(name) == "string"
}
// A `boolean | null` field type name — the niche FIELD (code 21): an i32 storage slot
// holding 0/1 with the spare value 2 = `null` (the field analogue of the sentinel local).
export function nameIsNulBool(name: string) {
  const b = nullablePartOf(name)
  b == "boolean"
}
// True if `name` is a 1-D `(boolean | null)[]` list — a LIST of the boolean sentinel
// niche. Its backing is the SAME `(array (mut i32))` as `boolean[]` / `i32[]`: each
// element is an i32 holding 0/1, with the spare value 2 = `null` (the list analogue of
// the scalar `boolean | null` niche — no box). So it rides the i32-list machinery
// (`nameIsI32Array`) rather than the union-box ref-list, and elements coerce/read
// through the sentinel (construct/`push`/index-assign seed `null → 2`; a read compares
// against 2). A nested `(boolean|null)[][]` / nullable `(boolean|null)[] | null` is NOT
// this (their element is a LIST, not the scalar niche) — only the single-level list.
export function nameIsNulBoolList(name: string) {
  const elem = listElemNameOf(name)
  if elem == "" { return false }
  nameIsNulBool(elem)
}
// True if `name` is a 1-D `(string | null)[]` list — a LIST of the string ref made nullable.
// Its backing is the SAME string list (`$mkListIdx` wrapper, `(ref null $aTypeIdx)` string backing)
// as `string[]`: a `string[]` element is ALREADY a NULLABLE ref at the wasm level, so the nullable
// element needs no new type — only `null → ref.null $aTypeIdx` on construct and the element READ
// (kept `(ref null)` = a `nulstr`, narrowed with `!= null`) differ. Rides the string-list machinery
// (`retStrArrFlag`, via `nodeTyArrayElemRepName` → "string"), NOT the union-box ref list. A nested
// `(string|null)[][]` / nullable `(string|null)[] | null` is NOT this (their element is a LIST).
export function nameIsNulStrList(name: string) {
  const elem = listElemNameOf(name)
  if elem == "" { return false }
  nameIsNulString(elem)
}
// True if `name` is a 1-D `(K | null)[]` list — a LIST of the literal-union atom made nullable via
// the i32 NICHE. Its backing is the SAME `(array (mut i32))` as `K[]` / `i32[]`: each element is an
// i32 holding a member id (≥ 0) with the spare value `-1` = `null` (the list analogue of the scalar
// `K | null` niche — no box). So it rides the i32-list machinery (`nameIsI32Array`), NOT the
// union-box ref-list: a member-literal element lowers as its atom id, a `null` element as `-1`, and a
// read compares against `-1` for the narrow. A nested `(K|null)[][]` / nullable `(K|null)[] | null` is
// NOT this (their element is a LIST, not the scalar niche) — only the single-level list.
export function nameIsNulLitUnionList(name: string) {
  const elem = listElemNameOf(name)
  if elem == "" { return false }
  nameIsNulLitUnion(elem)
}
// A NULLABLE LITERAL-UNION type name — `K | null` / `"a" | "b" | null` (the i32 atom NICHE): the
// member ids are interned ≥ 0 and the spare value `-1` encodes `null` (no box, like the boolean
// niche but with a NEGATIVE sentinel since atom ids start at 0). `canonEmitName` PRESERVES the
// litunion (`nulLitUnionPreserve`) so the members survive here — a `K | null` is detected by the
// alias non-null part (`nullablePartOf` → litunion), and the expanded inline form `"a"|"b"|null` by
// its all-quoted non-null members. Routes to `vtKindOfType` kind 0 (plain i32); `== null` is a
// `-1` compare and narrowing to the bare litunion is purely type-level (the i32 already holds a
// valid id in the non-null branch).
// It is the PREDICATE form of `nulLitUnionInnerName`, which is the primitive: the niche
// exists exactly when there IS a non-null litunion part to name, so one function decides
// both and they cannot disagree.
export function nameIsNulLitUnion(name: string) {
  nulLitUnionInnerName(name) != ""
}
// THE ONE HOME of the nullable-litunion niche. The NON-null part of a nullable-litunion
// name — the litunion alias (`K` of `K | null`) or the re-joined quoted members of the
// expanded inline form (`"a" | "b"` of `"a" | "b" | null`). "" when `name` is not a
// nullable litunion, which is also the "no" of the predicate above.
//
// This family was FOUR functions asking the SAME name the SAME question up to FIVE times:
// `nameIsNulLitUnion` scanned for the nullable split, then split the union in
// `nameHasNullMember`, then split it AGAIN in `nulLitUnionCore`; `nulLitUnionInnerName`
// called that whole cascade and then re-derived the nullable split and the union split for
// itself. The expanded form is decided by ONE pass over ONE split now — a top-level `null`
// member, and 2+ non-null members that are ALL quoted string literals — and the same pass
// builds the answer it was deciding about.
export function nulLitUnionInnerName(name: string) {
  // Two-part `K | null`: the non-null part is a litunion alias / inline union.
  const b = nullablePartOf(name)
  if b != "" {
    if nameIsLitUnionType(b) { return b }
    return ""
  }
  // Expanded multi-member form `"a" | "b" | null`: a top-level `null` member plus an
  // all-quoted-literal remainder (2+ members, so `nullablePartOf`'s two-part split missed
  // it). The literal test rejects on the FIRST unquoted member, exactly as `nulLitUnionCore`
  // did; reaching that reject before seeing the `null` member is the same answer, since both
  // conditions were required.
  const parts: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(name, parts)
  let lits = 0
  let hasNull = false
  let out = ""
  let i = 0
  while i < parts.length {
    const p = parts[i]
    if p == "null" {
      hasNull = true
    } else {
      if p.length < 2 { return "" }
      // The QUOTED-LEAF test at its home (`tyname.nameIsQuotedLeaf`, W3); this site's own
      // `length < 2` bound stays in front of it.
      if !nameIsQuotedLeaf(p) { return "" }
      lits = lits + 1
      if out == "" { out = p } else { out = out + " | " + p }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  if !hasNull { return "" }
  if lits < 2 { return "" }
  out
}
// A NULLABLE FUNCTION-VALUE type name — `((i32) => i32) | null` (kind 19): a
// `(ref null $cloStruct)` cell, the closure analogue of the nullable struct (kind 9). The
// non-null part must be a FUNCTION type, and the WHOLE name must NOT itself be a top-level
// function type — `((i32) => i32) | null` (parens preserved by `canonEmitName`, arrow
// nested at depth 1) is a nullable function, whereas `(i32) => i32 | null` (top-level
// arrow) is a function RETURNING a nullable (handled by `annRetKind`, not here).
export function nameIsNulClosure(name: string) {
  if annArrowAt(name) >= 0 { return false }
  const b = nullablePartOf(name)
  if b == "" { return false }
  // The non-null part must itself BE a closure — a TOP-LEVEL arrow once its disambiguating
  // grouping parens are peeled (`((i32) => i32)` → `(i32) => i32`). It is NOT enough to merely
  // CONTAIN a `=>`: `((i32) => boolean)[] | null` is a nullable ARRAY of closures and
  // `{f: () => i32} | null` a nullable STRUCT — the closure is nested in a composite, so the
  // whole is a nullable ref-array / nullable struct, not the closure niche (kind 19). Mis-
  // classifying them as nulclosure typed the field/binding slot `(ref null $cloStruct)` and the
  // construct stored a ref-array wrapper into it — invalid wasm.
  //
  // `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` IS "peel every fully-enclosing grouping-paren layer, then test for
  // a top-level arrow" — character-for-character the `annArrowAt(peelGroupParens(b))` this
  // line spelled out (its peel and `peelGroupParens` share `parenEnclosesWhole`; its arrow
  // scan and `annArrowAt` are the same walk), and it is the predicate the sibling
  // `nullClosureArrElem` already calls to ask the IDENTICAL question of the identical kind
  // of name — "is this nullable's non-null part a FUNCTION type".
  nameIsFuncTypeAtom(b)
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is a nullable function `(fn) | null`, else `0`
// — the closure dual of `retNulRefFlag`.
export function nulCloFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 {
    if nodeTyIsNulClosure(tyIx) { return 1 }
    return 0
  }
  if nameIsNulClosure(tyNameOf(tyIx)) { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `i32[] | null`, else `0` — the
// nullable-LIST dual of `retNulRefFlag`. Feeds vt kind 15 (a NULLABLE `(ref null
// $lTypeIdx)` slot: narrowing is type-level only, the slot stays nullable and
// each list-op read site recovers non-null — mirroring the host's lowering, see
// tests/cases/types/nullable-list-index.vl's header).
// The scalar type NAME used to classify annotation node `tyIx`, read STRUCTURALLY from the
// checker's `Ty` arena (`nodeTyPrimName`) when it recorded a type for the node, else falling
// back to the parser's synthetic annotation name (`tyNameOf`) for a node the checker did not
// record. Byte-identical to the pure-name form (a scalar's arena prim-name equals its
// annotation name), but reads the CHECKED type where available — a first step of C1 (thread
// the structured `Ty` to the emitter). The name fallback shrinks as more annotation-resolution
// sites populate `nodeTyIx`.
function nodeScalarName(tyIx: i32) {
  // `nodeTyWidenedRepName` (not the raw `nodeTyPrimName`) so a SINGLE literal / homogeneous
  // numeric literal union widens to its base scalar name here too — `retI64Flag`/`retF64Flag`/
  // `retF32Flag` then fire for a `: 1.5` / `: 7`-annotated param/return, matching the widened
  // value. It is a SUPERSET of `nodeTyPrimName` for prims (identical), adding the lit widening;
  // the extra "string" it yields for a string literal/atom matches none of the numeric/bool flags.
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 { return nodeTyWidenedRepName(tyIx) }
  ""
}
// The array ELEMENT-REP name for classifying annotation node `tyIx` — STRUCTURAL from the `Ty`
// arena (`nodeTyArrayElemRepName`) where the checker recorded a type, else derived from the
// synthetic name for a node it did not record. Byte-identical to the per-classifier `nameIsX[]`
// tests; the `ret*ArrFlag` family reads it (C1). The name fallback shrinks as coverage widens.
function arrElemRep(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 { return nodeTyArrayElemRepName(tyIx) }
  ""
}
export function retNulListFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 {
    if nodeTyIsNulI32List(tyIx) { return 1 }
    return 0
  }
  if nameIsNulI32List(tyNameOf(tyIx)) { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is the i32 array type (`i32[]`), else `0`.
// The functype/local valtype layer multiplexes the struct/array/i32 cases through one
// `kind` code, so this flat flag feeds the `kind == 2` (array) branch of `pushVT`. A REF
// array (`S[]`/`N[]`) is NOT this — it feeds `kind == 5` via `retRefArrFlag`.
export function retArrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if arrElemRep(tyIx) == "i32" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `string`, else `0`. A string is
// CURRENTLY the SAME `(array (mut i32))` of code points an array uses, so it feeds the
// `kind == 3` branch of `pushVT` (which encodes the identical array ref valtype).
export function retStrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  // An ALIASED literal-union return (`type K = "a" | "b"`; `pick(): K`) reps as an i32
  // ATOM — `vtKindOfType` derives its functype result i32 (`nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias`), so
  // its `"str"` return kind must agree. `nodeTyWidenedRepName` renders every litunion
  // "string" (the print-widening name), which flagged the atom return a string; every call-result
  // consumer (`fnRetString` → `exprString`, the inferred-return chain) then treated the
  // returned i32 atom as a string ref — `print(pick(ks))` / `useK(pick(ks))` routed it to
  // the string-ref path (invalid wasm). The atom widens to its member string at the print
  // seam (`exprIsLitAtom`), not by claiming a string RETURN. An INLINE litunion
  // (`"a" | "b"` written at the annotation) reps as the string ref (its functype result
  // is the `retStrFlag` fallthrough below), so it must stay flagged — the guard is
  // alias-only, matching `vtKindOfType`.
  if nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias(tyIx) { return 0 }
  if nodeTyWidenedRepName(tyIx) == "string" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `boolean`/`bool`, else `0`. A bool
// rides in an i32 at the wasm level — this feeds ONLY the `print` import choice
// (`fRetBool`), never a valtype.
export function retBoolFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeScalarName(tyIx) == "boolean" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is a `string | null` — the NULLABLE string
// ref `(ref null $aTypeIdx)`, kind 16. A `string` is the raw i32 array `(ref $aTypeIdx)`;
// the nullable form is the same backing made nullable (`null` is `ref.null`, `!= null`
// is `ref.is_null`). Checked in `vtKindOfType` BEFORE the bare `string` (kind 3).
export function retNulStringFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 {
    if nodeTyIsNulString(tyIx) { return 1 }
    return 0
  }
  // `nameIsNulString` IS this compare (the code-20 niche's one home) — the same
  // hand-copy `tyIsNulBool` carried for the boolean sibling.
  if nameIsNulString(tyNameOf(tyIx)) { return 1 }
  0
}
// Combine the struct/array/string flags for a type-annotation node at `tyIx` into the
// single `pushVT` `kind` code: `1` struct, `2` array, `3` string, `0` i32. (Struct
// wins if a name were somehow both — it cannot be, the lexemes are disjoint.) String
// is a distinct code from array purely for typedness clarity; `pushVT` emits the SAME
// array ref valtype for both, since a string IS the i32-array type here.
// STRUCTURAL (destringify): the annotation node at `tyIx` is an array whose ELEMENT is a
// NULLABLE closure (`(((i32) => boolean) | null)[]`) — a `TyArray` over a `TyNullable` over a
// `TyFunc`. The arena dual of `nullClosureArrElem(tyNameOf(tyIx)) != ""`: `nullClosureArrElem`
// answers the same question by string surgery (peel the `(...)[]` element, split the `| null`,
// re-test the non-null part for a top-level arrow). `nodeTyIsClosureArray` covers only the
// BARE-closure element, so this is the nullable-element arm of the same kind-5 classifier.
function tyIsNulClosureArrayNode(tyIx: i32) {
  const ty = nodeTyIxOf(tyIx)
  if ty < 0 { return false }
  const t = T.tys[ty]
  if t is TyArray {
    const e = t.aElem
    if e >= 0 {
      const et = T.tys[e]
      if et is TyNullable {
        const inner = T.tys[et.nInner]
        if inner is TyFunc { return true }
      }
    }
  }
  false
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is an ARRAY OF FUNCTIONS (`((i32) => i32)[]`),
// else `0` — the closure-element ref-list guard `vtKindOfType` checks before the kind-14
// function-type test.
export function nameIsClosureArrayTy(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeTyIxOf(tyIx) >= 0 {
    if nodeTyIsClosureArray(tyIx) { return 1 }
    // A NULLABLE-closure-element array (`(((i32) => boolean) | null)[]`, kind-5 niche):
    // `nodeTyIsClosureArray` has no nullable-element arm, so the SAME arena spine answers
    // it here (destringify) — a `TyArray` over a `TyNullable` over a `TyFunc`, replacing
    // the `nullClosureArrElem` string surgery over the annotation's rendered name.
    if tyIsNulClosureArrayNode(tyIx) { return 1 }
    return 0
  }
  if cloArrElemNameOf(tyNameOf(tyIx)) != "" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is an `f64[]` (the f64-element list), else
// `0`. Feeds the `kind == 12` (f64-list wrapper) branch of `fbValtype`/`pushVT`. Checked
// BEFORE the `retRefArrFlag`/`retArrFlag` cases since `f64[]` is an `[]` array name but
// is neither a struct/union ref list nor the i32 list. (Slice 4.)
export function retF64ArrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if arrElemRep(tyIx) == "f64" { return 1 }
  0
}
export function retF32ArrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if arrElemRep(tyIx) == "f32" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `i64[]` — the i64-list rep (kind 20).
export function retI64ArrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if arrElemRep(tyIx) == "i64" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `i64`, else `0`. A scalar i64
// param/result/local/global classifies as kind 10 (valtype `0x7e`); checked LAST
// (after every ref/array case) since `i64` is a plain TypeRef name like `i32`.
export function retI64Flag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeScalarName(tyIx) == "i64" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `f64`, else `0`. A scalar f64
// param/result/local/global classifies as kind 11 (valtype `0x7c`); checked LAST
// (alongside `retI64Flag`) since `f64` is a plain TypeRef name like `i32`. (Slice 3.)
export function retF64Flag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeScalarName(tyIx) == "f64" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is `f32`, else `0`. A scalar f32
// param/result/local/global classifies as kind 13 (valtype `0x7d`); checked LAST
// (alongside `retI64Flag`/`retF64Flag`) since `f32` is a plain TypeRef name like `i32`.
// (Slice 5.)
export function retF32Flag(tyIx: i32) {
  if nodeScalarName(tyIx) == "f32" { return 1 }
  0
}
// `1` if the type-annotation node at `tyIx` is a `string[]` (string-ref list), else `0`.
// Feeds the `kind == 7` (string-list wrapper) branch of `pushVT`. Checked BEFORE the
// `retRefArrFlag`/`retArrFlag` cases since `string[]` is an `[]` array name but is NOT a
// struct/union ref list nor the i32 list — it is the dedicated string-ref list.
export function retStrArrFlag(tyIx: i32) {
  if arrElemRep(tyIx) == "string" { return 1 }
  0
}
// The `array.new_fixed` operand cap engines enforce (V8/binaryen/wasmtime reject
// more than 10,000 operands). A literal longer than this cannot lower as one
// `array.new_fixed`: in a FUNCTION body it builds chunked (`array.new_default` +
// per-chunk `array.new_fixed`/`array.copy` — see `emitStr`/`emitArr`), and a
// GLOBAL initializer holding one is routed through the start function
// (`isConstInit` below returns false — a wasm constant expression has no locals
// to chunk through).
export const NEW_FIXED_MAX = 10000
// Whether the initializer expression at `exprIx` is a valid WasmGC *constant
// expression* — the forms `emitProgram` already lowers inline in the global section
// (`i32.const`, `struct.new`, `array.new_fixed`, the string array). Const forms:
// `NumLit` / `CharLit` / `BoolLit` (an `i32.const`), `StrLit` (a constant `array.new_fixed`
// over the code points), an `ArrayLit` whose elements are ALL const, an `ObjLit` whose
// field values are ALL const, and a `Paren` wrapping a const. EVERYTHING ELSE is
// non-const: an `Ident` (a `global.get` of another global), a `Member` / `Index` (a
// `struct.get` / `array.get` — the `curBuf = W.bytes` shape), a `Call`, a `BinExpr` /
// `Unary` (computed), an `IsExpr`. A literal past the `array.new_fixed` operand cap
// is non-const too — it must build CHUNKED through locals, which only a function
// body has (the raw `strText` length bounds the decoded length from above, so the
// `+ 2` quote allowance never under-routes a too-long string). CONSERVATIVE by
// construction: an unrecognized node falls through to the non-const `false`, so a
// form we have not proven const-safe is initialized via the start function rather
// than emitted as an invalid constexpr.
export function isConstInit(exprIx: i32) {
  if exprIx < 0 { return false }
  const e = P.nodes[exprIx]
  if e is NumLit { return true }
  if e is CharLit { return true }
  if e is BoolLit { return true }
  if e is StrLit { return e.strText.length <= NEW_FIXED_MAX + 2 }
  if e is Paren { return isConstInit(e.parInner) }
  if e is ArrayLit {
    if e.arrElems.length > NEW_FIXED_MAX { return false }
    let i = 0
    while i < e.arrElems.length {
      if !isConstInit(e.arrElems[i]) { return false }
      i = i + 1
    }
    return true
  }
  if e is ObjLit {
    let i = 0
    while i < e.objFields.length {
      const f = P.nodes[e.objFields[i]]
      if f is FieldInit {
        if !isConstInit(f.fiValue) { return false }
      } else {
        return false
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
    return true
  }
  false
}
// ── the ARRAY-NAME grammar: `nameIsArray` TESTS it, `arrElemNameRaw` CUTS it ───────────
//
// **BOTH HOMES ARE IN `typecheck.vl` (D-ARRDOWN), AND THE FULL HEADERS LIVE THERE**; this
// file republishes them at the top. Four of the checker's own functions (`nameToTyReal`,
// `canonEmitName`, `nulElemListAtomKind`, `litUnionArrayElemOf`) hand-wrote this
// two-character suffix test, and three of them the cut, because `typecheck.vl` cannot
// import THIS file. Only `i32[]` is emittable in this slice; a non-i32 element array is
// rejected by `checkArrName`. What follows are the LAYERS the emitter adds over them.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the array-name TEST's second spelling: `nameIsArray` with a NON-EMPTY
// element — i.e. the `n >= 3 && name[n - 2] == '[' && name[n - 1] == ']'` triple, which is
// the form nine of `emit_classify.vl`'s eleven hand-written copies took (`n > 2` and
// `n >= 3` are the same integer predicate). It is `nameIsArray` MINUS exactly one name,
// the degenerate `"[]"`, and it is precisely the guard half of `arrElemNameRaw(name) != ""`
// — the two agree by construction because the raw cut yields "" for a shorter name and for
// a non-array alike.
//
// This is a LAYER over `nameIsArray`, not a second copy of it: the character compares live
// once, at the home above, and this adds only the length bound. It exists because the
// alternative routing — testing `arrElemNameRaw(name) != ""` — allocates a slice to answer
// a boolean, and several of the sites that ask are per-struct/per-field inner loops the
// program has already paid an allocation regression to (`nameIsRefArray`'s own header
// records the `sNames[i] + "[]"` concat it deleted for exactly that reason).
export function nameIsElemArray(name: string) {
  if name.length < 3 { return false }
  nameIsArray(name)
}
// THE ONE HOME of the ARRAY-DIMENSION peel: `name` with EVERY trailing `[]` pair removed
// (`{f: i32}[][]` → `{f: i32}`, `S[]` → `S`, `S` → `S`). Unlike `arrElemNameRaw` this
// never answers "": a non-array name is its own leaf, which is what all five hand-written
// copies did (they are `while`-loops that simply do not iterate).
//
// Four copies live in `emit_classify.vl` (`internShapeArms`, `internNonLowerableFieldShapes`,
// `internShapeFieldElems`, `gaeEnsure`) and one in `emit_collect.vl`, each six or
// seven lines of the same `length > 2` + two char compares + slice. Three of the four then
// ask the SAME next question of the leaf (`leaf[0] == '{' && leaf[1] != '['`), which is why
// the drift risk here is real rather than theoretical: a peel that stopped one level short
// hands a `{…}[]` to a test that only accepts `{…}`.
//
// It is the CUT home applied to fixpoint, not a second copy of the cut: the loop body is a
// call to `arrElemNameRaw`, and it advances only while that call yields a non-empty element
// — so the degenerate `"[]"` terminates the peel rather than looping, exactly as the
// `length > 2` bound in every copy did. (`nameIsNestedUnionElemArray` below runs the same
// shape by hand for a different reason — it needs the LAST array-name rung, not the leaf —
// so it is not folded in here.)
export function arrLeafNameOf(name: string) {
  let leaf = name
  let done = false
  while !done {
    const nx = arrElemNameRaw(leaf)
    if nx == "" { done = true } else { leaf = nx }
  }
  leaf
}
// ── the PAREN grammar: `nameIsParenOpen` OPENS it, `nameIsParenSpanEnds` SPANS it ──────
//
// **BOTH HOMES ARE IN `typecheck.vl` (D-PARENDOWN), AND THE FULL HEADER LIVES THERE**; this
// file republishes them at the top. They are pure character grammars over a type-name STRING
// with no emitter state in them, so they had no reason to live in a module the CHECKER
// cannot import — and the checker hand-wrote the same three compares at six sites of its own
// because it could not reach them. **There is exactly one copy of this grammar in the
// compiler and it is not here.** (`emit_classify` imports the SPAN half from here — #1192
// routed seven sites to it — and gets `typecheck`'s definition through the re-export.)
//
// The 1-D LIST-ELEMENT peel `listElemNameOf` is republished with them: the trailing `[]` cut
// plus the ONE layer of disambiguating grouping parens a composite element carries
// (`(boolean|null)[]` → `boolean|null`, `K[]` → `K`), "" when `name` is not an array name.
// It is `arrElemNameRaw` plus `nameIsParenSpanEnds`, so it belongs beside them, and the
// checker's `nulElemListAtomKind` / `litUnionArrayElemOf` each carried a hand-written copy
// of it for want of an import.
//
// THE TWO WALKS ARE NOT ONE. `peelGroupParens` rides `typecheck.parenEnclosesWhole` and
// `normTypeAtom` below rides `tyGroupWrapsWhole`; they differ on a group that NEVER CLOSES
// (`tyGroupEndIndex` returns -1), where the former answers FALSE and the latter TRUE. That
// difference is load-bearing and documented at `peelGroupParens`. Merging them by eyeball is
// the mistake this comment exists to prevent.
//
// ── the SCALAR-ARRAY classifiers: an ELEMENT-name compare, not a whole-spelling one ────
//
// **THE FAMILY-WIDE NOTE, written once here and pointed at from the four siblings below**
// (`nameIsStringArray`, `nameIsF64Array`, `nameIsF32Array`, `nameIsI64Array`).
//
// Each of these five used to ask a WHOLE-TYPE-SPELLING structural equality — `name ==
// "f64[]"`, `name == "i32[]" || name == "boolean[]"` — which decides a COMPOSITE type by
// matching its rendered characters. That is the destringify rule's clearest violation: the
// `[]` is STRUCTURE and the `f64` is a NAME, and a whole-spelling compare fuses the two into
// one opaque character match, so neither half can be seen, shared, or repaired.
//
// Split at that seam they are `arrElemNameRaw(name) == "<elem>"`: the ARRAY grammar's ONE
// CUT (`typecheck.vl`, D-ARRDOWN/#1175 — the trailing-`[]` cut whose nine hand-written
// copies in THIS file are what buying it a home cost) followed by a BARE PRIM-NAME equality
// on the leaf, which the rule explicitly allows — a name is a name.
//
// EXACTLY EQUIVALENT, provably and totally: `arrElemNameRaw` returns "" for a non-array
// name (and "" is none of the six leaf spellings), else `name` minus its final two
// characters — so `arrElemNameRaw(n) == "f64"` holds iff `n` is 5 characters that are
// `f64` then `[` then `]`, i.e. iff `n == "f64[]"`. No input distinguishes the two forms.
//
// AND IT PUTS THE NAME SIDE IN THE SHAPE THE ARENA SIDE ALREADY SHIPS. `arrElemRep(tyIx)`
// (above) is the arena's element-rep query, and the whole `ret*ArrFlag` family is already
// written as `arrElemRep(tyIx) == "f64"`. These five were its NAME-side twins, surviving
// only because their callers hold a spelling rather than a node. Written this way the two
// halves of one classifier read identically and can no longer drift on what an f64 array IS.
//
// DECLINED, measured: `listElemNameOf` (the cut PLUS one naive grouping-paren strip) would
// additionally accept `(f64)[]`. Its divergence population is EMPTY — `(f64)[]`, `(f32)[]`,
// `(i64)[]` and `(string)[]` already lower correctly WITHOUT the wider cut, in binding,
// param and return position, so the widening has no witness to license it. That is the
// measured BEHAVIOUR; where exactly the parenthesized element stops being visible to these
// classifiers was NOT traced, so do not quote a mechanism here. Per the D-SHAPEFIELD
// precedent (unreached is not licenced), the FAITHFUL cut ships and the widening is filed.
// The measurement itself is pinned by `tests/cases/arrays/paren-wrapped-scalar-elem.vl`.
//
// True if the array type-name `name` is `i32[]` (the value-element list whose backing
// is the raw `(array (mut i32))`). A `boolean[]` shares this exact rep (a boolean is an
// i32 0/1 at the wasm level), so it rides the same i32-list machinery — only the element
// PRINT formatting recovers boolean-ness (`listElemIsBool`). A `T[]`/`N[]` (struct/union
// element) is NOT this.
export function nameIsI32Array(name: string) {
  // The two i32-backed leaf spellings share one cut — the family note above.
  const elem = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  if elem == "i32" || elem == "boolean" { return true }
  // `(boolean | null)[]` shares the exact i32-list backing: each element is an i32 0/1
  // with the spare value 2 = `null` (the sentinel niche). Only the `null → 2` element
  // coercion + the sentinel-aware read/print differ (see `nameIsNulBoolList`).
  if nameIsNulBoolList(name) { return true }
  // A literal-union array (`K[]`, or an inline `("a"|"b")[]`) — its elements are interned-i32
  // ATOMS, so it shares the i32 backing/rep EXACTLY like `i32[]`. (Element construction seeds
  // `pendingLitUnion` so the member literals lower as their ids; reads yield the atom.)
  if nameIsLitUnionArray(name) { return true }
  // A `(K | null)[]` shares the exact i32-list backing: each element is an i32 member id (≥ 0)
  // with the spare value `-1` = `null` (the atom-niche analogue of `(boolean | null)[]`). Only the
  // `null → -1` element coercion + the sentinel-aware read differ (see `nameIsNulLitUnionList`).
  if nameIsNulLitUnionList(name) { return true }
  false
}
// True if `name` is `<litunion>[]` — an array whose element type is a literal union. The element
// name is `name` minus the trailing `[]` (a parenthesized inline union keeps its parens, which
// `nameIsLitUnionType` -> `nameIsInlineLitUnion` tolerates via the union split).
export function nameIsLitUnionArray(name: string) {
  const elem = listElemNameOf(name)
  if elem == "" { return false }
  nameIsLitUnionType(elem)
}
// True if `name` is a 2-D array whose ELEMENT is an i32-backed scalar list — `i32[][]`,
// `boolean[][]`, or a literal-union `K[][]`. All three share the `i32[][]` rep EXACTLY: the
// element is the i32-list wrapper (`lTypeIdx`, ref-list element kind 4), because `i32[]`,
// `boolean[]` and a litunion `K[]` share ONE i32[] backing (`nameIsI32Array`). One predicate for
// the three ref-array classifiers (`nameIsRefArray` / `refArrElemKind` / `refArrElemName`), which
// each special-cased only the literal `"i32[][]"` name — so the boolean/atom leaf (`boolean[][]`,
// `K[][]`, the R4 fuzz family) now falls out of the SAME i32-list-element arm without a fourth
// drifting enumeration. A deeper `i32[][][]` is NOT this (its element `i32[][]` is itself a ref
// array, kind 9 via the recursion) — only a single i32-backed leaf list qualifies.
export function nameIsI32ListArray(name: string) {
  const elem = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  if elem == "" { return false }
  return nameIsI32Array(elem)
}
// ── the MAP-NAME OPENER moved down too (D-MAPOPENDOWN) ────────────────────────────────
//
// **`nameIsMapOpen` IS `typecheck.vl`'s AND ITS FULL HEADER LIVES THERE**; this file
// republishes it at the top, so `emit_classify`/`emit_collect` compile untouched. This
// header used to name two copies to re-derive in `typecheck.vl` — `nameToTyReal`'s map arm
// and `mapShapeKeyName` — and **both are gone**: D-MAPSPELL (#1224) replaced them with the
// checker's own map grammar, which made this definition a SECOND complete home of an
// identical two-line predicate rather than the only one. Deleting it is the same down-move
// the paren / array / shape / brace / generic-application grammars took, and it finishes the
// hand-off D-MAPSPELL wrote at the map grammar's home.
//
// **THIS IS NOT `nameIsMap`, AND THE TWO MUST NOT BE MERGED BY EYEBALL.** `nameIsMap` below
// is that predicate MINUS the arrays: a list-of-maps `{[string]:i32}[]` opens `{[` but is a
// REF ARRAY, not a bare map, so `nameIsMap` rejects it while the opener accepts it. A caller
// asking "does this name START a map" — a union ARM that must not be swallowed by the
// map-VALUE recursion, whose whole union name also opens `{[` — wants the OPENER; a caller
// asking "is this name a bare map" wants `nameIsMap`. Routing an arm test to `nameIsMap`
// would silently narrow it to the non-array arms and re-open the garbled-map-value bug the
// arm test exists to prevent. A third occurrence, `nameIsStructWithMapField`'s `{[`-anywhere
// scan below, is a SUBSTRING search rather than a name-shape predicate and is not this.
//
// True if the type-name `name` is a string-keyed MAP `{[string]: i32}` (G8) — the
// synthetic name `parseType` builds for an index-signature type, which starts with
// `"{["`. Only `{[string]: i32}` is emittable in this slice (the self-host checker's
// scope-chain element); any other index signature still parses to this shape but the
// map lowering assumes string keys + i32 values. A trailing `[]` (a list-of-maps
// `{[string]:i32}[]`) is NOT a bare map — that is handled by the ref-array path.
export function nameIsMap(name: string) {
  // The MAP-NAME OPENER at its home, carrying its own `length >= 2` bound (this spelled the
  // same two character compares out inline behind the same bound).
  if !nameIsMapOpen(name) { return false }
  // A trailing `[]` makes it a LIST of maps, not a bare map — the array-name grammar's
  // TEST half, at its home. (This spelled the same two character compares out inline; at
  // length >= 2 the two readings accept exactly the same names.)
  !nameIsArray(name)
}
// True if `name` is a UNION whose FIRST atom is a map (`{[string]: V} | …`) — a name that both
// STARTS with `{[` (so `nameIsMap` matches it) AND has 2+ top-level atoms. Such a name is a
// variant BOX, not a bare map: it must NOT be classified as a map local / return / value / field /
// list element. A bare map has ONE top-level atom (the inner `|` of a union-VALUED map like
// `{[string]: C | D}` sits inside the braces, so `splitUnionAtoms` keeps it a single atom).
export function nameIsMapMemberUnion(name: string) {
  if !nameIsMap(name) { return false }
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(name, atoms)
  atoms.length >= 2
}
// True if the MAP atom `mapAtom` (`{[string]: V}`) is boxable as a UNION arm. A map arm ALWAYS
// boxes as its VALUE-KIND map STRUCT ref riding the box anyref, tagged via `mapSlotTag` — for
// EVERY value kind: SCALAR / STRING / mono (`i32`/`boolean`/atom → `$mStructIdx`; `string`/`i64`/
// `f64`/`f32` → a per-value map struct over a scalar vals list) AND ref-VALUE (`{[string]: S}`
// struct, `{[string]: {[..]}}` nested, `{[string]: A|B}` union, `{[string]: () => T}` closure,
// `{[string]: S[]}` ref-list — a per-value map struct over a REF vals list). The value-kind map
// struct dedups across the union-box seam because BOTH the box side (`markMapUnionArms` →
// `mvShapeOfValName`, the authoritative `collectA` intern) and the narrowed read (`mvSlotOf…` →
// `mapTypeIdxOf`) key the SAME mv slot off the SAME arm atom via `mapValNameOf` — one slot, one
// `$mapStruct` heap type (the #860 map-struct-dedup invariant, extended to the union-box path). An
// unrepresentable value type (`mvValKindOfName == -3`) cannot cross the box: `mapShapeOfExpr`
// returns -3, so `emitUnionCoerce`'s `mSlot >= -1` gate fails and the arm stays a LOUD reject —
// never invalid wasm. (Lifts #865's scalar/string-only restriction; the ref-value hazard was the
// intermediate binding / field / return classifiers mis-typing a map-member union as a bare map,
// now guarded by `nameIsMapMemberUnion` at every `paramMap` / `retMapFlag` / `nameFieldCode` site.)
export function mapArmValueBoxable(_mapAtom: string) { true }
// True if `name` is a BARE map (`{[string]: V}`) — the composite `nameIsMap(name) &&
// !nameIsMapMemberUnion(name)`, which is the ONE question five call sites in this file
// and `emit_collect.vl` were each spelling out. `nameIsMap` is a two-character probe
// (`{` then `[`), so a map-FIRST union (`{[string]: i32} | boolean`) passes it and every
// consumer that wants "a map, not a box" has to subtract the false positive by hand;
// #1127's audit measured 24 `nameIsMapMemberUnion` sites and found the corrective-guard
// reading holding at 21 of them. This is that reading, named once.
//
// It is also the shape the ARENA answers directly: `ty is TyMap` is true for a bare map
// and false for a `TyUnion` with a map arm — that is `typecheck.nodeTyIsMap`, and
// D-BAREMAP moved every ANNOTATION-NODE consumer onto it (`retMapFlag`,
// `retIsMapLocalAnnot`, `letIsMap`, `letMapShapeOf`, `paramMap`). What is left here is
// the calls that hold a STRING and no node — an atom peeled out of a union, a slice of
// a bigger spelling, `nameIsMapArray`'s element — where there is no type to ask.
export function nameIsBareMap(name: string) {
  nameIsMap(name) && !nameIsMapMemberUnion(name)
}
// True if the type-ANNOTATION node at `tyIx` is a NULLABLE bare map (`{[string]: V} | null`) —
// the ARENA dual of `nulMapInnerName(<the node's rendering>) != ""`, and the nullable twin of
// `nodeTyIsMap`. A `TyNullable` over a `TyMap`: the `| null` peel and the bare-map test the
// name pair spells as two scans are ONE walk down two variant tags, with no union split and
// no substring. (`typecheck.vl` carries `nodeTyIsNullableStruct`, the same shape over `TyObj`;
// the map sibling lives here because this is where its only consumers are.)
export function tyIsNulBareMap(tyIx: i32) {
  const ty = nodeTyIxOf(tyIx)
  if ty < 0 { return false }
  const t = T.tys[ty]
  if t is TyNullable {
    if t.nInner < 0 { return false }
    const it = T.tys[t.nInner]
    return it is TyMap
  }
  false
}
// True if the array type-name `name` is a string-keyed-MAP list `{[string]:i32}[]` —
// a ref list whose ELEMENT is the map struct (`mStructIdx`). It is a `nameIsMap` name
// with a trailing `[]`. The scope chain (`typecheck.vl`'s `scopes: {[string]:i32}[]`)
// is exactly this. Routed through the ref-list machinery as element-kind `3`.
export function nameIsMapArray(name: string) {
  // The bare element (without the trailing `[]`) must be a BARE map name — NOT a map-member union
  // (`({[string]: i32} | boolean)[]` whose paren-stripped element `{[string]: i32}|boolean` starts
  // with `{[` too): that is a union-BOX ref list (kind 2), not a list-of-maps (kind 3).
  // The cut is RAW (`arrElemNameRaw`, not `listElemNameOf`) because that is what this
  // function did before the merge — NOT because the difference is observed. Sabotage S5
  // installs the paren-STRIPPING cut here and reddens 0 of 1,398 corpus files and 0 of
  // 38,400 fuzz programs, so the two readings have a measured-EMPTY divergence population
  // on both channels. Unreached is not the same claim as licenced; the faithful cut ships.
  const bare = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  if bare == "" { return false }
  nameIsBareMap(bare)
}
// True if `name` is an ARRAY OF FUNCTIONS — `((i32) => i32)[]`: a trailing `[]` whose
// element is a fully-parenthesized FUNCTION type, the arrow NESTED (depth > 0). A
// TOP-LEVEL arrow (`(i32) => i32[]`) is a function RETURNING a list, NOT an array of
// functions — excluded via `annArrowAt` (the same disambiguation as the list-return
// name guard). The element heap type is the shared closure fat-pointer struct
// (`cloStructIdx`); the ref-list element kind is 5.
export function nameIsClosureArray(name: string) {
  const n = name.length
  if n < 4 { return false }
  // The array-name grammar at its two homes. The TEST stays in front of the `annArrowAt`
  // walk exactly as the inline compares did — running that walk on a non-array name would
  // be a work regression — and the CUT stays behind it, so no name pays for a slice it is
  // about to be rejected for.
  if !nameIsArray(name) { return false }
  if annArrowAt(name) >= 0 { return false }
  const elem = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  // The PAREN grammar's OPEN half at its home. The separate `elem.length < 1` reject this
  // replaced is the home's own bound, so the empty element still declines before the
  // `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` walk below rather than indexing out of bounds.
  if !nameIsParenOpen(elem) { return false }
  // The element must be a genuine FUNCTION type — a TOP-LEVEL arrow (paren-aware, after
  // peeling grouping parens). A `strContains(elem, "=>")` test also matched a UNION whose
  // FIRST member is a closure (`(() => i64) | i64`, whose `|` sits at the top level with the
  // arrow NESTED inside the first member) and mis-typed it as a closure ref list — a raw
  // closure element slot the boxed value could not store (invalid wasm). `nameIsFuncTypeAtom`
  // distinguishes `(i32) => C | S` (closure returning a union, kind 5) from `(() => i64) | i64`
  // (value-union box element, kind 2) by where the arrow sits.
  nameIsFuncTypeAtom(elem)
}
// The non-null FUNCTION-TYPE atom of a NULLABLE-closure array element
// (`(((i32) => boolean) | null)[]` → `((i32) => boolean)`), or "". A
// `<closure> | null` element is a nullable NICHE over the shared closure
// fat-pointer (`(ref null $cloStruct)` — the backing a plain closure element
// already uses), NOT a union box — so such an array is a CLOSURE ref list
// (kind 5) whose slot keys the full `"<closure> | null"` name: the index read
// stays nullable (`rlElemNullable`) for the `e != null` narrow, exactly as the
// nullable-struct element (`nullStructArrElem`) does over its struct heap.
export function nullClosureArrElem(name: string) {
  const elem = parenUnionArrElemName(name)
  if elem == "" { return "" }
  const nn = nullablePartOf(elem)
  if nn == "" { return "" }
  if nameIsFuncTypeAtom(nn) { return nn }
  ""
}
// The ELEMENT function-type atom of a closure-array name, PLAIN or nullable-element:
// `((i32) => R)[]` → `(i32) => R`, `(((i32) => R) | null)[]` → `((i32) => R)`, else "".
// The one home of an idiom six call sites carried by hand (`emit_base` ×1,
// `emit_collect` ×5): the nullable-element form first (its element keeps the `| null`
// grouping parens, so the plain test declines it), then the plain form's `[]` peel.
//
// The two are the SAME question — "does this name's element ride the closure
// fat-pointer" — and every caller wanted one of exactly two answers from it: the
// element NAME (to key a `$fnsig`) or the BOOLEAN. `cloArrElemNameOf(n) != ""` is that
// boolean exactly: `nullClosureArrElem` returns a non-empty function-type atom or "",
// and `nameIsClosureArray` requires `n >= 4` with a trailing `[]`, so its peel is
// non-empty whenever it fires. Both orders of the old disjunction agree — the tests are
// disjoint (a `| null` element is not a `nameIsFuncTypeAtom`) and both are pure.
export function cloArrElemNameOf(name: string) {
  const nul = nullClosureArrElem(name)
  if nul != "" { return nul }
  if nameIsClosureArray(name) { return arrElemNameRaw(name) }
  ""
}
// The VALUE-union element name of a parenthesized union-array annotation:
// `(string|i32)[]` → `string|i32` (`canonEmitName` keeps a union element's
// grouping parens), or "" when `name` is not a value-union array name.
// Registration-independent (classifies from the atom set, not `unNames`).
export function parenUnionArrElemName(name: string) {
  if name.length < 5 { return "" }
  // A TOP-LEVEL arrow makes this a FUNCTION TYPE returning an array (`(i32) => (string | {w:
  // i32})[]`), NOT a value-union array. Without this guard the `name[0]=='('` / `name[len-3]==')'`
  // checks below match the closure name's OUTER parens (first `(` of the param list … last `)` of
  // the return union), capturing an unbalanced inner (`i32)=>(string|{w:i32}`) whose miscounted `|`
  // makes `unionMemberCount > 1` — mis-typing the whole closure LOCAL as a union-box ref list.
  // (`nameIsClosureArray` carries the same guard for the closure-element-array case.)
  if annArrowAt(name) >= 0 { return "" }
  // The array-name grammar's TEST half, at its home (this spelled the same two character
  // compares out inline; `name.length >= 5` is already established above).
  if !nameIsArray(name) { return "" }
  // The PAREN grammar's SPAN, asked of the ELEMENT rather than of the whole name — the whole
  // of the OPEN + `length - 3` CLOSE pair this spelled out inline.
  //
  // D-PARENHOME declined to route these two lines, on the reasoning that "`nameIsParenSpanEnds`
  // asks about the LAST character and would answer for the `]`". That is true of the WHOLE
  // name and false of its ELEMENT, which is the object the `length - 3` spelling was always
  // talking about: `nameIsArray` is established one line above, so `arrElemNameRaw(name)` is
  // `name` minus exactly its final two characters, giving `elem[0] == name[0]` and
  // `elem[elem.length - 1] == name[name.length - 3]`. The pair is therefore
  // `nameIsParenSpanEnds(elem)` character for character, on every input rather than on every
  // input tried — and the home's own `length >= 1` bound is covered by `name.length >= 5`
  // above, which makes `elem` at least 3 characters long.
  if !nameIsParenSpanEnds(arrElemNameRaw(name)) { return "" }
  // The whole name must be ONE atom — a multi-arm union that merely STARTS with a paren
  // group and ENDS with `[]` (`(boolean|string)[] | (i32|f64)[]`) would otherwise slice an
  // UNBALANCED "inner" (`boolean|string)[]|(i32|f64`) and mis-classify the whole union as
  // a single union-array, derailing its registration.
  if unionMemberCount(name) > 1 { return "" }
  const inner = name.slice(1, name.length - 3)
  // A `(boolean | null)[]` element is the i32 SENTINEL niche, not a box: it reps as a
  // plain i32 list (`nameIsI32Array`), so it must NOT read as a union-box ref-array
  // element here. Return "" so `nameIsRefArray` / `refArrElemKind` skip it and it lands
  // on the i32-list path. (Scalar `boolean | null` is likewise a niche, not a value
  // union — `isValueUnionName` already excludes it.)
  if nameIsNulBool(inner) { return "" }
  // A `(string | null)[]` element is the string ref niche (a `(ref null $aTypeIdx)` string), not a
  // box: it reps as a plain string list (`nameIsNulStrList`), so it must NOT read as a union-box
  // ref-array element here. Return "" so `nameIsRefArray` / `refArrElemKind` skip it and it lands on
  // the string-list path. (Scalar `string | null` is likewise a niche, not a value union.)
  if nameIsNulString(inner) { return "" }
  // A `(K | null)[]` element is the litunion atom NICHE (an i32 member id with `-1` = `null`), not a
  // box: it reps as a plain i32 list (`nameIsNulLitUnionList` → `nameIsI32Array`), so it must NOT read
  // as a union-box ref-array element here. Return "" so `nameIsRefArray` / `refArrElemKind` skip it
  // and it lands on the i32-list path. (Scalar `K | null` is likewise a niche, not a value union.)
  if nameIsNulLitUnion(inner) { return "" }
  if isValueUnionName(inner) { return inner }
  // A STRUCT / heterogeneous union element (`(C | S)[]`, `(A | i32)[]`): the parens are
  // annotation grouping only and the element slots hold the SAME `{tag, payload}` boxes a
  // value-union element does (element kind 2, heap `uBoxIdx`). A top-level `|` in a type
  // position is always a union, so `unionMemberCount > 1` distinguishes it from a
  // parenthesized non-union element (`((i32) => i32)[]`, no `|`).
  if unionMemberCount(inner) > 1 { return inner }
  ""
}
// True if `name` is a NESTED (2-D+) array whose innermost 1-D leaf is a parenthesized
// value/variant/map union (`({...}|i32)[][]`, `(i64|boolean)[][]`). Peels the OUTER `[]`
// levels down to the last single-bracket `(...)[]` leaf and confirms it is a union-element
// array (`parenUnionArrElemName`). The pin predicate `synthRetAnnots` uses for a nested
// union-element array lambda return — the 1-D `parenUnionArrElemName` arm rejects it (its
// `name[len-3] == ')'` guard fails on a `][]` suffix). A top-level arrow (a closure returning
// an array) is excluded.
export function nameIsNestedUnionElemArray(name: string): boolean {
  if annArrowAt(name) >= 0 { return false }
  const inner = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  if !nameIsArray(inner) { return false }
  let leaf = inner
  let done = false
  while !done {
    // `leaf` is an array name at every iteration (the line above establishes it, and the
    // loop only advances on a `nameIsArray` hit), so the home's cut IS this slice.
    const nx = arrElemNameRaw(leaf)
    if nameIsArray(nx) { leaf = nx } else { done = true }
  }
  parenUnionArrElemName(leaf) != ""
}

// True if the type-name `name` is exactly `string`. A VL string is CURRENTLY an
// `array i32` of code points (`docs/strings-design.md`), so it shares the array heap
// type — hence string use also sets `aUsed` and string valtypes reuse the array ref.
// (`string[]` is NOT this — it ends in `[]`, so `nameIsArray` catches it first and
// `checkArrName` rejects it as a non-i32 element array.)
export function nameIsString(name: string) { name == "string" }
// True if the type-name `name` is exactly `string[]` — a growable list whose elements
// are string refs. It reuses the map keys list types (the `{backing,len,cap}` wrapper
// over a `(ref null $aTypeIdx)` string backing); field code `6`.
export function nameIsStringArray(name: string) {
  // Element-name compare over the array grammar's one cut — see the family note at
  // `nameIsI32Array`.
  if arrElemNameRaw(name) == "string" { return true }
  // `(string | null)[]` shares the EXACT string-list backing (`$mkListIdx` wrapper, `(ref null
  // $aTypeIdx)` string backing — a `string[]` element is already a nullable ref). So it rides the
  // string-list machinery (param slot / `.length` / construct / arg-coerce) uniformly; only the
  // element READ diverges (kept `(ref null)`, intercepted by `exprNulStrArray` before the non-null
  // recovery) and a `null` element/push/assign lowers as `ref.null`.
  nameIsNulStrList(name)
}
// True if the type-name `name` is exactly `f64[]` — a growable list whose elements are
// scalar f64 (Slice 4). Its backing is `(array (mut f64))` (`fa64TypeIdx`); its wrapper
// is the `{backing,len,cap}` triple over that f64 backing (`fl64TypeIdx`). Distinct from
// `i32[]` so element reads/writes use the f64 element store.
// These three are the family note's purest form: one cut, one leaf name. They are also the
// three `emit_rewrite.vl`'s scalar-leaf-list return arm used to hand-write inline as
// `ctx == "i64[]" || ctx == "f64[]" || ctx == "f32[]"` — three copies of three one-line
// homes, in a file that already imported this one.
export function nameIsF64Array(name: string) { arrElemNameRaw(name) == "f64" }
export function nameIsF32Array(name: string) { arrElemNameRaw(name) == "f32" }
// The i64 sibling of `nameIsF64Array`: an `i64[]` annotation (backing `ia64TypeIdx`,
// wrapper `il64TypeIdx` — the i64 element store).
export function nameIsI64Array(name: string) { arrElemNameRaw(name) == "i64" }
// True if the node at `ix` is a struct `type` declaration (a single-level narrowing
// helper, so callers avoid an inline `is TypeDecl` on the wide `Node` union).
export function nodeIsTypeDecl(ix: i32) {
  const s = P.nodes[ix]
  if s is TypeDecl { return true }
  false
}
// True if `name` is the name of a FuncDecl ANYWHERE in the arena (top-level or a
// nested/lifted function). Used at `collectS` time — BEFORE `buildFnMap` populates
// `fnNames`, so `fnIndexOf` is not yet usable — to classify an anon-struct field
// whose value is a bare function name (`{ go: add }`). A direct arena scan, so it
// sees a function declared after the literal too.
export function nameNamesFunction(name: string) {
  if name == "" { return false }
  let i = 0
  while i < P.nodes.length {
    const n = P.nodes[i]
    if n is FuncDecl {
      if n.fnName == name { return true }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}
// ── the SHAPE-NAME grammar: the OPENER and the WIDE SPAN are BOTH one module down ──────
//
// **`nameIsShapeOpen` (D-SHAPEDOWN) AND `nameIsBraceSpanEnds` (D-BRACEDOWN) LIVE IN
// `typecheck.vl`, AND THEIR FULL HEADERS LIVE THERE**; this file republishes both at the top.
// Only their CONJUNCTION stays here, because it is the one brace question no checker site
// asks: the checker's three `{`…`}` endpoint tests all OMIT the `name[1] != '['` conjunct and
// so accept a MAP name, which is the WIDE predicate and is now homed as such below the
// emitter, next to the map grammar it has to stay distinct from.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the inline-shape ENDPOINT span: `nameIsShapeOpen` AND the final character
// is `}`. This is a LAYER over the opener, not a second copy of it — the character compares
// live once, above.
//
// **THIS IS NOT `emit_classify.nameIsWholeSpanShape`, AND THE TWO MUST NOT BE MERGED BY
// EYEBALL.** That function is this predicate PLUS `tyGroupWrapsWhole(name)`, a depth walk
// proving the group opened at index 0 is still open at the last character. The endpoints
// alone do not prove that: `{a:{b:i32}}|{c:i32}` opens with `{`, is not a map, and ends
// with `}`, yet the leading group closes early — this home answers TRUE and
// `nameIsWholeSpanShape` answers FALSE. **10 sites carry this exact endpoint predicate and
// every one of them would be TIGHTENED by routing to the walk instead** (`emit_base`
// `unionStructAliasShape`/`isStructAtom`, `emit_collect.collectVariantFields`,
// `emit_classify` ×5, `emit_rep` ×2). None was tightened; the walk keeps its one caller and
// this home takes the ten. Narrowing any of them is a behaviour change owing its own graded
// population, not a refactor.
//
// The SECOND non-merge, and the one that bites in THIS file: `length >= 2 && name[0] == '{'
// && name[name.length - 1] == '}'` WITHOUT the `name[1] != '['` conjunct is a DIFFERENT
// predicate — it accepts a map name, since `{[string]:i32}` opens with `{` and ends with
// `}`. That one is `typecheck.nameIsBraceSpanEnds`, republished at the top of this file, and
// this function is written in terms of it: the two are `BRACE-SPAN · NOT2ND` and
// `BRACE-SPAN`, which is why one can delegate to the other without either widening.
//
// #1186 named FOUR `emit_base` functions as carrying the wider predicate — `annObjFieldSplit`,
// `canonBareShapeName`, `nameIsStructWithUnionField`, `nameIsStructWithLitUnionField` — for a
// tree-wide count of 9. **`canonBareShapeName` was NOT one of them and never was**: it carried
// `if name[1] == '[' { return false }` on the line after its endpoint test at `fa5a6bb` itself,
// making it this predicate EXACTLY, and it now says so by calling this function. The wider
// predicate's real population is **8**, and the four-in-`emit_base` is three.
export function nameIsShapeSpanEnds(name: string) {
  if !nameIsBraceSpanEnds(name) { return false }
  nameIsShapeOpen(name)
}
// The inline-shape synthetic name of a STRUCT-ALIAS union decl — a `UnionDecl`
// with exactly ONE variant that is an inline object shape (`{a:i32,b:i32}`,
// not a map `{[…]}`) — or "". The shape a canonicalized object intersection
// (`type AB = {a} & {b}` → the merged single variant) leaves behind: it is a
// plain named STRUCT at runtime, so `collectS` interns it and `collectU`
// skips it.
export function unionStructAliasShape(variants: string[]) {
  if variants.length == 1 {
    const v = variants[0]
    if nameIsShapeSpanEnds(v) { return v }
  }
  ""
}
// The `<`-scan and the generic-application SPAN (`gaeLtAt` / `annGenAppSpanEnds`) moved one
// module DOWN to `typecheck.vl` (D-GENAPPDOWN) and are republished at the top of this file;
// their full headers live at the home. `gaeSplitArgs` below stays: it is a reading of
// `tyTopLevelSplit`, which is this file's.
// Split a type-argument list at its TOP-LEVEL commas (`<>`/`{}`/`[]`/`()`
// nest); empty parts drop (a tolerated trailing comma) — the `dropEmpty` reading of
// the shared depth walk.
export function gaeSplitArgs(s: string, out: string[]) {
  tyTopLevelSplit(s, ',', true, out)
}
// True when a declared type-parameter NAME binds nothing because a BUILTIN type spelling
// wins it: `type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }` has no parameter, it has a field of type `i32`.
//
// This is the CHECKER's leaf-resolution ORDER read as a predicate, and that order is the
// whole rule: `tsLeafTy`/`nameToTy` ask `primTyOfName` FIRST, the live parameter binding
// (`tpEnvTyOfName`) SECOND, and the declared struct/union registry (`declaredTyOfName`)
// THIRD. So a parameter named for a primitive is shadowed BY the primitive, while a
// parameter named for a DECLARED type shadows it — and both directions are observable:
// given `type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }` the checker reports `Box<string>` as `{v: i32}`,
// and given `type S = {n: i32}` + `type Box<S> = { v: S }` it reports `Box<i32>` as
// `{v: i32}`. Substituting at a primitive-named parameter therefore hands the emitter a
// field type the checker never agreed to, and two answers for one field is invalid wasm
// on a program `vl check` accepts.
//
// The bound is `primTyOfName`'s accept set — i32 / i64 / f64 / f32 / boolean / string /
// void / null. `valueAtomKind` is that set MINUS `void`, which it does not carry because
// `void` is a type spelling and not a VALUE; the one name is added back so the two sets
// coincide exactly rather than nearly. `valueAtomKind` also claims the array spellings
// (`i32[]` and friends), and those cannot decide anything here: this predicate is only
// ever asked about a name that a LEAF compared equal to, and a leaf carries no `[]`.
//
// It is deliberately NOT the bound `genTyParamArr` (`emit_classify.vl`) uses for its own
// shadow guard. That arm exists to spare `checkArrName` a name it would wrongly reject,
// so its bound is `checkArrName`'s accept set; this one exists to agree with the CHECKER
// about what a field's type IS, so its bound is the checker's own resolution order. Same
// defect shape, different neighbour to agree with.
//
// A parameter named for a name the CANON PASS REWRITES used to survive this predicate, and
// no longer does — the name it had to see was gone by the time it was asked. `type Id = i32`
// + `type Box<Id> = { v: Id }` + `const b: Box<string> = { v: "z" }` was `vl check` rc=0 with
// `b` reported as `{v: string}` (the parameter binds, per the order above) and an emitted
// module that did not parse, because `typecheck.canonEmitName` rewrote the field annotation
// `Id` to `i32` before `gaeCollectDecls` recorded it. The population was never just the
// transparent alias: `type Id = 0 | 1` and the one-member `type Id = 1` shadow through
// `numLitUnionAliasName`, a DIFFERENT canon arm, and fail identically — while a declared
// STRUCT or UNION shadow always ran, because canon rewrites neither. `gaeCollectDecls` now
// records the DECLARED spelling for exactly the fields `gaeAnnHasParam` claims
// (`typecheck.declAnnRawTyName`), so this predicate is asked about `Id` again.
//
// THE FuncDecl HALF ASKS THIS SAME QUESTION AT `monoTyParamOf`, and the note that used to
// stand here — "the ANCHOR is the inferred-RETURN classification" — was WRONG in both of
// its claims and is recorded as refuted rather than deleted. Measured over a 6x6
// parameter-name x ARGUMENT-type grid, the axis is not the rep: it is whether the
// ARGUMENT's type equals the shadowed primitive. `function id<i64>(v: i64)` RUNS when
// handed a genuinely `i64`-typed argument and is invalid wasm when handed an `i32` one, and
// the disassembly reads `(param i32) (result i64)` — the RETURN is the half that agrees
// with the checker and the PARAM is the erased one, the opposite way round. The earlier
// per-row single literal made the diagonal look green for the reason the diagonal is
// always green. See `monoTyParamOf`'s own guard.
function gaeParamShadowed(name: string) {
  if name == "void" { return true }
  valueAtomKind(name) >= 0
}
// The LIVE parameter slots of a generic alias — the declared names that actually BIND,
// paired with the application arguments that bind them. A parameter whose name is a
// primitive spelling binds nothing (`gaeParamShadowed`, which is the checker's
// `primTyOfName`-first resolution order read as a predicate), so it is dropped from BOTH
// lists together and the substitution below never sees it.
//
// Filtering here rather than inside the walk is what keeps the shadow rule in ONE place:
// `monoSubstAnn` is shared with the FuncDecl half, whose own shadow question is a
// different arm at a different site, and a guard buried in the shared walk would answer
// both with one rule.
function gaeLiveParams(
  pnames: string[],
  args: string[],
  outP: string[],
  outA: string[],
) {
  let i = 0
  while i < pnames.length {
    if !gaeParamShadowed(pnames[i]) {
      outP.push(pnames[i])
      outA.push(args[i])
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  0
}
// Would the substitution below CHANGE this field's type name — i.e. does the annotation
// reach a live type parameter anywhere in its structure? THE substitution's decision,
// published so the one other place that has to ask it asks it rather than re-deriving it:
// `emit_collect.gaeDeclFieldTyName` uses it to decide whether a field's DECLARED spelling
// is the one to record. Two copies of this question would be two answers, and the defect
// #1238 closed is exactly that.
//
// It REPLACES the `gaeParamSlotOf(arrLeafNameOf(raw), …)` gate that shipped with the
// sidecar. That gate read the array grammar and nothing else, so a parameter sitting
// behind ANY other constructor — `Id | null`, `{a: Id}`, `Inner<Id>` — was reported as
// "the substitution does not land here", the canon'd `i32` was recorded, and the field
// interned at the ALIAS's body type while the checker had bound it to the ARGUMENT.
export function gaeAnnHasParam(tn: string, pnames: string[]) {
  const lp: string[] = []
  const la: string[] = []
  gaeLiveParams(pnames, pnames, lp, la)
  monoAnnHasTyParam(arrLeafNameOf(tn), lp)
}
// Substitute a declared type-PARAMETER name in a field's type name with the application's
// argument name, THROUGH THE TYPE'S STRUCTURE: `T` → `i32`, `T[][]` → `i32[][]`,
// `T | null` → `i32 | null`, `{a: T}` → `{a: i32}`, `(T | null)[]` → `(i32 | null)[]`.
//
// It is `monoSubstAnn` — the generic-instantiation walk this file already owns for the
// FuncDecl half — asked with the alias's live parameters as the bindings, NOT a third
// hand-rolled ladder. Peeling one constructor is what a ladder can do; the population is
// every constructor the annotation grammar has, and each rung someone forgets to write is
// a field that interns at the alias's BODY type while the checker reads the ARGUMENT.
// #1234 bought the `[]` rung (`type Box<T> = {v: T[][]}` was an emit error at every
// instantiation), and the `| null` and `{…}` rungs were the identical defect asked of two
// more constructors — 26 `vl check`-clean invalid-wasm cells across a 15x8 declaration x
// constructor grid, every one with a green non-shadowing control.
//
// THE `[]` RUN NO LONGER HAS TO STAY HERE, and the sentence this replaces said it did.
// `arrLeafNameOf` strips whole `[]` pairs off the END, so the leaf is a PREFIX of `tn` and
// the remainder is precisely the repetitions to re-attach — the dimensions are carried as
// the slice they already are, never rebuilt from a count. That is now ALSO the bottom arm
// of `monoSubstAnn` itself (character for character, and the gate above is the bottom arm
// of `monoAnnHasTyParam`), so this peel and its `gaeAnnHasParam` twin are a redundant outer
// layer rather than the only home of the depth. They are kept because they are inert —
// peeling a leaf the walk would peel again changes no answer — and retiring them is a
// separate byte-inert move that should be measured on its own.
//
// What is STILL true is the narrower claim underneath: the run must not be pushed into
// `monoTyParamOf`, whose branch in `monoSubstAnn` writes `b + "[]"` and so answers a
// two-dimensional pattern with a one-dimensional name. See `monoTyParamOf`'s header, which
// carries #1242's 14-cell measurement and the correction to what it was read as proving.
//
// THE GATE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION. An annotation that reaches no
// live parameter is returned VERBATIM rather than walked, because the walk RE-RENDERS what
// it descends (a union rejoins on `|`, a shape rebuilds `{n:t,…}`) and a re-rendered
// spelling that differs from the recorded one by a single character is a second intern key
// for one type. Only a name the substitution actually changes may be rebuilt.
export function gaeApplyFieldTy(tn: string, pnames: string[], args: string[]) {
  const lp: string[] = []
  const la: string[] = []
  gaeLiveParams(pnames, args, lp, la)
  const leaf = arrLeafNameOf(tn)
  if !monoAnnHasTyParam(leaf, lp) { return tn }
  const s = monoSubstAnn(leaf, lp, lp, la)
  // "" is `monoSubstAnn`'s UNBOUND answer, which cannot arise here — every name in `lp`
  // is bound by construction — so this and the unchanged-leaf test are the
  // un-substitutable fallbacks, not cases.
  if s == "" { return tn }
  if s == leaf { return tn }
  s + tn.slice(leaf.length, tn.length)
}
// Lexicographic `a < b` over two strings by code point (VL has no `<` on `string`).
// Compares char-by-char: at the first differing position the smaller code point wins;
// if one is a prefix of the other, the shorter is smaller. Equal strings are NOT `<`.
export function strLess(a: string, b: string) {
  let i = 0
  const na = a.length
  const nb = b.length
  while i < na && i < nb {
    const ca = a[i]
    const cb = b[i]
    if ca < cb { return true }
    if ca > cb { return false }
    i = i + 1
  }
  na < nb
}
// True if union `name` has at least one NON-null value-atom member (`i32`/`f64`/
// `boolean`/`string`/…) — true for a pure value union AND a MIXED struct+scalar union,
// false for an all-struct union. Gates the scalar value-boxing in `emitUnionCoerce`.
export function unionHasValueAtom(name: string) {
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(name, atoms)
  let i = 0
  while i < atoms.length {
    const k = valueAtomKind(atoms[i])
    if k >= 0 && k != 6 { return true }
    // A litunion-ALIAS atom (`K0 | {w: i32}`) rides the box as its member-string atom
    // (the `K0 | i64` value-union rule) — it IS a value-boxable carrier, and the coerce
    // ladder below the gate already resolves the alias arm for a string carrier. Without
    // this an alias + all-struct union (no plain scalar sibling) took the all-struct raw
    // pass-through, returning the RAW member string into the box result — INVALID-WASM.
    if nameIsLitUnionType(atoms[i]) { return true }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}
// True if the value union `name` contains the member atom `atom`.
export function unionHasAtom(name: string, atom: string) {
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(name, atoms)
  let i = 0
  while i < atoms.length {
    if atoms[i] == atom { return true }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}
// `peelGroupParens` — peel every fully-enclosing balanced grouping-paren layer from `name`
// (`(() => i64)` → `() => i64`), returning a non-parenthesized name unchanged. **THE HOME IS
// IN `typecheck.vl` (D-PARENDOWN)** — it is the SPAN predicate plus `parenEnclosesWhole`'s
// walk, both of which live there, and `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` (also there) hand-wrote this exact
// loop because it could not import the home from a module above it. Republished at the top of
// this file, which is what keeps `emit_classify`/`emit_collect`/`emit_rep` untouched.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the union-ARM normalization: trim ASCII whitespace, then peel ONE
// fully-wrapping `(...)` pair. A closure member arrives from `splitUnionAtoms` WRAPPED
// (`(()=>i64)` — an unwrapped top-level arrow would bind the trailing `|` into its
// RETURN) and may carry the `| `-split's spaces; the bare annotation spelling has
// neither, so a key derived from an arm must be normalized to byte-match the one derived
// from the annotation.
//
// `emit_collect.vl`'s `peelWrapType` and `emit_classify.vl`'s `unionArmSigKey` prelude
// were CHARACTER-IDENTICAL copies of this — the former's own header said so ("Shares the
// exact normalization `unionArmSigKey` applies before keying"), which is the strongest
// possible statement that they must not be allowed to drift. `emit_base` is the lowest
// module both reach, so the home is here.
//
// The wrap test is `tyGroupWrapsWhole`'s — the same "does the leading group close EARLY"
// question `emit_collect.nameIsSingleShape` asked of a `{…}` group, including the reading
// of a group that never closes at all (both answer "it wraps"; the checker's
// `parenEnclosesWhole` answers the opposite, which is why `peelGroupParens` is NOT this).
// The copy here counted `(`/`)` ONLY, and its own header defended that as load-bearing —
// "a `>` in a `$fnsig` key or a `tyToStr` render must not be able to unbalance it". That
// defence does not survive contact with the shared ladder: `tyGtIsClose` already makes a
// `>` with no OPEN `<` inert, so `ii>i` and `->` cannot unbalance the home either. Probe
// P2 measures the widening (a stray unmatched `}`/`]` inside the parens is the only shape
// the two ladders can disagree on) at 0 disagreements over the corpus and the fuzz corpus.
// THE CHEAP REJECT IS PART OF THE HOME. When the five out-of-file consumers of the retired
// `emit_classify.monoUnwrapParens` moved here, the walk count went UP: that copy demanded
// BOTH a leading `(` AND a trailing `)` before walking, while this one demanded only the
// leading `(` — so every `(i32) => i32`-shaped name (leading `(`, no trailing `)`) paid a
// whole `tyGroupWrapsWhole` walk to be told what its last character already said. Measured
// over the corpus and the fuzz corpus: 496 such walks (113 + 383). Restoring the reject is
// #1144's rule — keep the home AND the short-circuit — and it is also the only place this
// function could return NONSENSE: with no trailing `)`, `tyGroupWrapsWhole` answers TRUE
// only for a group that NEVER CLOSES, and the peel below then slices a REAL character off
// the end (`(abc` → `ab`). That population is measured EMPTY (0 of 19,680 invocations,
// corpus + fuzz, with the enclosing branch proven reached 496 times), so the reject is a
// work recovery whose behaviour change no channel can observe.
// AND SO IS THE NO-OP TRIM. D-MONOPAREN + D-ARMPAREN moved fourteen more call sites onto
// this home and took its invocation count from 1,606 corpus / 48,351 fuzz to 40,109 /
// 1,240,834 — a 25× move that turns an allocation from noise into a cost: `arm.slice(lo,
// hi)` with `lo == 0 && hi == arm.length` builds a whole new string to say "nothing was
// trimmed". THE TRIM IS NOT A MINORITY, IT IS EMPTY: a fire probe reads **0 of 1,280,943
// invocations** (40,109 + 1,240,834) on which either bound moved, and because a zero that
// large is a bug report about the wire until the wire is proved, the INVERTED wire (print
// when the trim was NOT needed) fires 40,109 / 40,109 on the corpus. So every arrival is a
// bare annotation spelling with no surrounding whitespace; the `| `-split arms that
// motivated the two loops reach no channel this compiler is measured on. The guard is the
// same #1144 move as the trailing-`)` reject one paragraph up — keep the home AND the
// short-circuit — and it is answer-exact by construction: `s.slice(0, s.length)` IS `s`.
// The loops STAY: "unreached on two channels" licenses skipping the allocation, not
// deleting the contract.
export function normTypeAtom(arm: string) {
  let lo = 0
  let hi = arm.length
  while lo < hi && (arm[lo] == ' ' || arm[lo] == '\t') { lo = lo + 1 }
  while hi > lo && (arm[hi - 1] == ' ' || arm[hi - 1] == '\t') { hi = hi - 1 }
  let t = arm
  if lo != 0 || hi != arm.length { t = arm.slice(lo, hi) }
  // The PAREN grammar's SPAN half at its home — the CHEAP REJECT the paragraph above argues
  // for, now named. The walk stays here and stays `tyGroupWrapsWhole`: it is NOT
  // `peelGroupParens`'s `parenEnclosesWhole` (they disagree on a never-closing group), which
  // is exactly why the home carries the endpoints and neither walk.
  // …and the CUT is `tyname.groupInnerOf`, the delimiter-blind home (W1). The BALANCE WALK
  // stays out of it on purpose: this is the FOURTH distinct guard the home's call sites put
  // in front of the same three characters of arithmetic (the other three being
  // `nameIsParenSpanEnds`, `nameIsShapeSpanEnds` and the OPEN-only `nameIsShapeOpen`), so a
  // cut that folded in any one of them would be wrong for the rest.
  if nameIsParenSpanEnds(t) {
    if tyGroupWrapsWhole(t) { t = groupInnerOf(t) }
  }
  t
}
// The pipe-joined complement of `set` (a union name / narrowed sub-set string)
// with the atom `removed` taken out — the ELSE-branch narrowing of `is removed`. The
// equality is grouping-paren-insensitive (`peelGroupParens`), so `is () => i64` strips the
// `(() => i64)` member `splitUnionAtoms` produced — otherwise the closure arm survives the
// complement and the else-branch reads the raw box where its remaining scalar arm is expected.
// PURE-FUNCTION MEMO: the narrowing passes call this millions of times per
// self-compile (~25% of compile time — every `if`-chain scan re-derives the
// same else-branch complements), over a SMALL set of distinct `(set, removed)`
// pairs. The result depends on nothing but the two strings, so the memo needs
// no invalidation — it persists across programs. The DOMINANT caller removes
// `"null"` (the else-branch of an `is null` test), so that case keys on `set`
// alone (`raftNullKeys` — no key-string concat on the hot path); every other
// `removed` rides the generic pair key, which length-prefixes `set`
// (`<len>:<set><removed>`) so no separator collision can conflate two pairs.
// Values live in a side pool (`raftVals`) because "" is a VALID result (the
// last atom removed) — the `{[string]: i32}` index maps keep a real miss
// (`?? -1`) distinguishable.
const raftNullKeys: {[string]: i32} = Map()
const raftKeys: {[string]: i32} = Map()
const raftVals: string[] = []

function removeAtomFromGo(set: string, removed: string) {
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(set, atoms)
  const rmv = peelGroupParens(removed)
  // Atoms are VERBATIM contiguous slices of `set` separated by single `|`s
  // (see `splitUnionAtoms`), so joining KEPT atoms with `|` equals slicing the
  // kept RUNS out of `set` directly — one slice per run of consecutive
  // survivors (≤2 in the common one-atom removal) instead of two concats per
  // atom, the same slice-over-per-atom-concat pattern as the splitter itself.
  let out = ""
  let runStart = -1 // char index where the current kept run begins, -1 = none
  let pos = 0 // char index of the current atom's start within `set`
  let i = 0
  while i < atoms.length {
    if peelGroupParens(atoms[i]) != rmv {
      if runStart < 0 { runStart = pos }
    } else if runStart >= 0 {
      // The run ends BEFORE this removed atom's separator (`pos - 1`).
      if out != "" { out = out + "|" }
      out = out + set.slice(runStart, pos - 1)
      runStart = -1
    }
    pos = pos + atoms[i].length + 1
    i = i + 1
  }
  if runStart >= 0 {
    if out != "" { out = out + "|" }
    out = out + set.slice(runStart, set.length)
  }
  out
}

export function removeAtomFrom(set: string, removed: string) {
  if removed == "null" {
    const hit = raftNullKeys[set] ?? -1
    if hit >= 0 { return raftVals[hit] }
    const out = removeAtomFromGo(set, removed)
    raftNullKeys[set] = raftVals.length
    raftVals.push(out)
    return out
  }
  const mk = i32ToStr(set.length) + ":" + set + removed
  const hit = raftKeys[mk] ?? -1
  if hit >= 0 { return raftVals[hit] }
  const out = removeAtomFromGo(set, removed)
  raftKeys[mk] = raftVals.length
  raftVals.push(out)
  out
}
// True if `name` is declared by a top-level struct `type X = {…}` (`TypeDecl`). Used
// during `collectU` — BEFORE `collectS` runs — so it scans the arena directly rather
// than reading the not-yet-populated `sNames` table.
export function isDeclaredStructName(stmts: i32[], name: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < stmts.length {
    const s = P.nodes[stmts[i]]
    if s is TypeDecl {
      if s.tdName == name { return true }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}
// True if the atom `a` is a STRUCT variant — a declared `type X = {…}`, or an INLINE
// object shape `{x:i32}` (its fields parse from its own text; a map shape `{[…]}` is
// not). The per-member test shared by `isVariantBoxUnion` and the registration loop.
export function isStructAtom(stmts: i32[], a: string) {
  if isDeclaredStructName(stmts, a) { return true }
  nameIsShapeSpanEnds(a)
}
// True if `name` is a VARIANT-BOX union this emitter interns: two+ non-null members,
// every member a struct variant OR a value-atom (`i32`/`string`/…) OR a REF-ELEMENT array
// (`S[]`, `(() => f64)[]`, `(C | D)[]`), with AT LEAST ONE struct OR ref-array. Covers the
// all-struct union (`A | B`, `A | B | null`), the MIXED struct + scalar union (`A | i32`,
// `A | B | i32 | f64 | boolean | string | null`), AND unions with a ref-array member
// (`S[] | {w:i32}`, `(() => f64)[] | i64`) — all ride the `uBoxIdx` `{tag, value}` box:
// structs as variant-struct payloads, scalars as value-box payloads, ref-arrays as the
// reflist WRAPPER ref on the anyref, tag-discriminated. A PURE value union (`i32 | string`)
// has no struct/ref-array, so it is handled earlier by `isValueUnionName`; a single struct +
// `null` (`A | null`) has one non-null member, so it stays the kind-9 niche nullable ref.
// ONE walk of `name`, not two. `unionMemberCount` is the COUNTING DUAL of
// `splitUnionAtoms` — the identical depth ladder, the identical quoted-atom skip, the
// identical top-level-arrow stop — so asking the count and then asking for the atoms of
// the SAME name walked it twice for one answer; `atoms.length` IS that count.
export function isVariantBoxUnion(stmts: i32[], name: string) {
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(name, atoms)
  isVariantBoxUnionAtoms(stmts, atoms)
}
// The ATOM-taking form, for a caller that has ALREADY split the same name. The split is
// the expensive half of the predicate (a whole depth walk of the name), and it is pure —
// so `registerInlineUnion`, which splits `name` into `matoms` at its top and then asked
// this question of that SAME `name` at TWO of its dispatch arms, was paying for THREE
// walks where one answers. #1147 filed this as hand-off 5 with the count measured but not
// taken; it is taken here, and the invocation counters show it (`splitUnionAtoms` and
// `tyTopIndexOf` both fall, with the classifier's own answer unchanged on every file).
export function isVariantBoxUnionAtoms(stmts: i32[], atoms: string[]) {
  if atoms.length < 2 { return false }
  let structAtoms = 0
  let refArrAtoms = 0
  let mapAtoms = 0
  let nonNull = 0
  let j = 0
  while j < atoms.length {
    if atoms[j] != "null" {
      nonNull = nonNull + 1
      if isStructAtom(stmts, atoms[j]) {
        structAtoms = structAtoms + 1
      } else if valueAtomKind(atoms[j]) >= 0 || nameIsLitUnionType(atoms[j]) {
        // A value atom (scalar / string / scalar-element list [kinds 7-10] / closure) — it
        // boxes into a shared value box or rides the anyref, tagged via `scalarTagOf`. Checked
        // BEFORE the array test so a scalar-element list (`i32[]`, `string[]`) keeps its
        // value-atom rep instead of being mis-taken for a ref-element array.
        // A litunion-ALIAS atom (`K0` in `K0 | {w: i32} | f64`) rides the box as its
        // member-string atom too (the `K0 | i64` value-union rule); the annotated
        // named-fn spelling of such a union already lowers, so the box gate admits it.
      } else if nameIsMap(atoms[j]) {
        // A MAP arm (`{[string]: string}`, `{[string]: f64}`): not a struct (a map shape carries no
        // field set) nor a value atom, so it boxes as its MAP STRUCT ref riding the box anyref,
        // tagged via `mapTagOf` — the direct analogue of the ref-array arm below. Checked BEFORE
        // `nameIsArray` (a bare map ends in `}`, not `]`, so they are disjoint). Only a SCALAR /
        // string / mono value is boxable; a REF-VALUE map arm (`{[string]: S}` / nested / union /
        // closure) has a heap-type-identity hazard in the box composite, so the WHOLE union is not a
        // box union — it stays a LOUD reject (the registration guard fails loudly, never invalid wasm).
        if !mapArmValueBoxable(atoms[j]) { return false }
        mapAtoms = mapAtoms + 1
      } else if nameIsArray(atoms[j]) {
        // A REF-ELEMENT array arm (`S[]`, `(() => f64)[]`, `(C | D)[]`): not a scalar-element
        // list (those are value atoms, handled above), so it boxes as its reflist WRAPPER ref
        // riding the box anyref, tagged via `refArrTagOf`.
        refArrAtoms = refArrAtoms + 1
      } else {
        // Neither a struct variant, a value-atom, a map, nor a ref-element array — not boxable.
        return false
      }
    }
    j = j + 1
  }
  (structAtoms >= 1 || refArrAtoms >= 1 || mapAtoms >= 1) && nonNull >= 2
}
// The synthetic type-name string of the annotation node at `tyIx` (a `TypeRef`), or
// "" if `tyIx` is unset / not a type name. Lets the inline-union scan read a param /
// return annotation's name without a `P.nodes[...] is TypeRef` round-trip at the call
// site (this slice cannot narrow there).
export function tyNameOf(tyIx: i32) {
  if tyIx >= 0 {
    const ty = P.nodes[tyIx]
    if ty is TypeRef { return ty.tyName }
  }
  ""
}
// Push `atom` onto `atoms` only if absent (dedup, preserving first-seen order).
export function pushUniqueAtom(atoms: string[], atom: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < atoms.length {
    if atoms[i] == atom { return 0 }
    i = i + 1
  }
  atoms.push(atom)
  0
}
// The number of NON-`null` members in the pipe-joined set `set`.
export function nonNullMemberCountOf(set: string) {
  const atoms: string[] = []
  splitUnionAtoms(set, atoms)
  let n = 0
  let i = 0
  while i < atoms.length {
    if atoms[i] != "null" { n = n + 1 }
    i = i + 1
  }
  n
}
// True if the `NumLit` lexeme `t` carries a `.` (a float literal). Mirrors the
// checker's literal-default rule (`.`→f64). (Slice 3.)
export function numTextIsFloat(t: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < t.length {
    if t[i] == '.' { return true }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}
// True when the integer lexeme `t` is plain DECIMAL (digits/underscores, no `0x`/`0b`/`0o`
// radix prefix) — the form `ieeeBytes` can re-encode directly as an `f64.const`/`f32.const`.
export function numLexIsDecimal(t: string) {
  if t.length >= 2 && t[0] == '0' {
    const p = t[1]
    if p == 'x' || p == 'X' || p == 'b' || p == 'B' || p == 'o' || p == 'O' {
      return false
    }
  }
  true
}
// The `memProp` of a Member expression at `exprIx` (or "").
export function callMemProp(exprIx: i32) {
  const e = P.nodes[exprIx]
  if e is Member { return e.memProp }
  ""
}
// The `$fnsig` signature KEY a closure-pinned parameter carries — the part after
// the `"=>"` of a monomorphized instance's synthetic annotation (`"=>ii>i"` →
// `"ii>i"`), or "" when `name` is not such a param of function `fnIx`.
// Top-level (paren/brace/bracket depth 0) index of `=>` in a synthetic function-type
// name, or -1.
//
// This was the EIGHTH hand-written copy of the file's bracket ladder, and the last one
// outside the two homes. It differed from the FIND walk in exactly one thing — the
// arrow's two-character lookahead — so the lookahead moved INTO the home as the `'='`
// token and the copy became this line. The grouper arms were already character-identical
// (the `||` chains list the same three pairs in a different order, which the depth
// counter cannot see). It now names the checker's home directly: the local FIND wrapper
// it used to go through was a frame that only permuted arguments.
export function annArrowAt(name: string) { tyTopIndexOf(name, '=', 0, 0) }
// THE ONE HOME of "the RETURN of a function-type name": the text after a TOP-LEVEL `=>`
// (`(i32) => S[]` → `S[]`), or "" when `name` carries no top-level arrow.
//
// Five sites in `emit_collect.vl` spelled this out as `annArrowAt(n)` followed by
// `n.slice(at + 2, n.length)` — two of them calling `annArrowAt` TWICE on the same name
// for one answer (`collectA`'s TypeRef arm). The arrow index itself is never what those
// callers wanted; the RESULT NAME is, and holding the index in a local is what let the
// duplicate scans hide. A caller that also needs the result's grouping parens peeled
// wraps this in `normTypeAtom` — the peel is a SEPARATE question and stays at the
// call site, so this home cannot silently widen a raw-slice consumer.
//
// The gate is `>= 1`, matching the `fa >= 1` the function-type consumers use: an arrow at
// index 0 would mean a name beginning `=>`, which no renderer produces.
// The CUT is `tyname.fnRetTextOf` (W4), which takes the index this function just found.
export function annRetNameOf(name: string) {
  const at = annArrowAt(name)
  if at < 1 { return "" }
  fnRetTextOf(name, at)
}
// THE ONE HOME of the FUNCTION-TYPE decomposition `(p1, p2) => ret`: fills `outParams`
// with the top-level parameter annotations and returns the RETURN annotation, or "" when
// `ann` is not a fully-parenthesized function type (no top-level arrow, or the arrow is
// not preceded by the param list's own `(`…`)`).
//
// The generic-instantiation trio in this file — `monoAnnHasTyParam`, `monoSubstAnn` and
// `monoBindFromAnn` (which decomposes BOTH the pattern and the actual) — carried FOUR
// copies of this same three-part test plus its two slices. They are the same grammar by
// construction: each exists to walk the other's output.
//
// The empty param list is NOT split (`() => R` yields no params). `annSplitParams` pushes
// one EMPTY part for an empty input, so the two copies that guarded on
// `innerFn.length > 0` are the correct reading — `monoSubstAnn` would otherwise
// substitute the empty part, get "" back and report the whole annotation unbindable. The
// third copy (`monoBindFromAnn`) omitted the guard and so paired a one-element `[""]`
// against another; that recursion binds nothing (`monoTyParamOf("")` is "" and
// `annArrowAt("")` is -1), which is why unifying on the guarded reading is byte-inert.
export function annFnDecompose(ann: string, outParams: string[]) {
  const fa = annArrowAt(ann)
  if fa < 1 { return "" }
  // The PAREN grammar's OPEN half at its home; `fa >= 1` above already implies the bound.
  // The CLOSE test stays inline — this is the ARROW-relative spelling, closing the param
  // list at `fa - 1`, where the LAST character belongs to the return annotation.
  if !nameIsParenOpen(ann) { return "" }
  if ann[fa - 1] != ')' { return "" }
  // Both CUTS are `tyname.fnParamsTextOf` / `fnRetTextOf` (W4), given the index above.
  const inner = fnParamsTextOf(ann, fa)
  if inner.length > 0 { annSplitParams(inner, outParams) }
  fnRetTextOf(ann, fa)
}
// THE ONE HOME of the GENERIC-APPLICATION decomposition `Head<a1, a2>`: fills `outArgs`
// with the top-level type arguments and returns the HEAD name, or "" when `ann` is not an
// application. The same generic-instantiation trio carried FOUR copies of the
// `ann[n-1] == '>'` + `gaeLtAt` + `gaeSplitArgs(ann.slice(lt+1, n-1))` triple.
//
// The three-part TEST is `annGenAppSpanEnds`, one module down (D-GENAPPDOWN) and republished
// at the top of this file; `gaeLtAt` is re-asked here rather than threaded out, and that costs
// a second scan only on a name that already ends in `>` — every other name is rejected by the
// character compare before either scan runs.
// Both CUTS are `tyname.gaeHeadNameOf` / `gaeArgsTextOf` (W2), which take the `<` index
// this function already holds. The ARGUMENT cut spelled its endpoint against the HOISTED
// length `n` — one of the two writings whose length expression #1250 named as the
// normalisation the census must apply and had not.
export function annGenAppDecompose(ann: string, outArgs: string[]) {
  if !annGenAppSpanEnds(ann) { return "" }
  const lt = gaeLtAt(ann)
  gaeSplitArgs(gaeArgsTextOf(ann, lt), outArgs)
  gaeHeadNameOf(ann, lt)
}
// THE ONE HOME of the inline OBJECT-SHAPE decomposition `{a:T,b:U}` in the generic
// instantiation layer: fills `outNames` / `outTexts` with the top-level field names and
// field type TEXTS, returning `1` for an object shape, `0` for a MAP (`{[`) and `-1` when
// `ann` is not a `{…}` group at all.
//
// `monoAnnHasTyParam` and `monoSubstAnn` carried this same four-part test plus its
// `gaeSplitArgs` + `indexOf(":")` cut — D-GRAMHOME's third mono grammar, filed as its
// hand-off 3 and taken here. The tri-state is what the two callers actually differ on: a
// MAP annotation is not descended by either, but one answers `false` for the whole
// function while the other returns the annotation UNCHANGED, so "not an object" and "a map
// object" have to stay distinguishable.
//
// A malformed part (no `name:` prefix) pushes an EMPTY name, `shapeInnerFieldSplit`'s
// convention: `monoAnnHasTyParam`'s `ci > 0` skip and `monoSubstAnn`'s `ci <= 0` bail are
// both exactly `outNames[i] == ""`. It deliberately splits with `gaeSplitArgs` (empty parts
// DROPPED) rather than reusing `shapeInnerFieldSplit` (empty parts KEPT): the two
// conventions disagree on a trailing comma, and only the dropping one preserves what these
// two callers do today.
export function annObjFieldSplit(
  ann: string,
  outNames: string[],
  outTexts: string[],
) {
  // The BRACE-SPAN home decides "is this a `{…}` group at all", and the MAP-OPEN home tells
  // the two positive answers apart — the tri-state's whole character surgery, at two homes.
  // Both spellings are exact: the endpoint pair plus its length bound IS `nameIsBraceSpanEnds`,
  // and once that holds, `ann[1] == '['` IS `nameIsMapOpen` (the length bound and the `ann[0]`
  // compare are already established). The `>= 3` conjunct the map test carried was inert —
  // at length 2 the character at index 1 is the closing `}` the line above just demanded, so
  // it can never be `[`.
  if !nameIsBraceSpanEnds(ann) { return -1 }
  if nameIsMapOpen(ann) { return 0 }
  const fields: string[] = []
  // The GROUP-INTERIOR cut at its home (`tyname.groupInnerOf`, W1). This site spelled it
  // against a HOISTED length — `const n = ann.length` at the top, then `ann.slice(1, n - 1)`
  // — which is why the #1239 census scored it in a separate row from the eighteen it filed
  // under W1: the census normalises the RECEIVER but not the length EXPRESSION. Routing it
  // retires the hoist, which had no other reader.
  gaeSplitArgs(groupInnerOf(ann), fields)
  let j = 0
  while j < fields.length {
    const ci = fields[j].indexOf(":")
    if ci <= 0 {
      outNames.push("")
      outTexts.push("")
    } else {
      outNames.push(fields[j].slice(0, ci))
      outTexts.push(fields[j].slice(ci + 1, fields[j].length))
    }
    j = j + 1
  }
  1
}
// Split the INNER text of an inline object shape (`{…}`'s contents — `a:i32,b:{c:f64}`)
// into PARALLEL field-NAME / field-TYPE-TEXT lists at depth 0.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the shape layer's field scanner FOR THE MODULES THAT REACH IT —
// `nameIsStructWithUnionField` / `nameIsStructWithLitUnionField` here, and
// `shapeHasCloField` / `collectShapeVariantFields` in `emit_collect.vl`. `emit_base` is
// the lowest module those importers already depend on, so the home belongs here.
//
// ⚠️ THIS HEADER HAS NOW BEEN WRONG THREE TIMES, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THAT IS THE
// POINT OF THE WARNING IT ALREADY CARRIED. An early version claimed
// `emit_classify`'s private copy had already retired into this one — it had not. The
// version before this one claimed the opposite: "**`emit_classify.vl` still defines a
// PRIVATE `shapeInnerFieldSplit` with SIX callers**", filed as a measured hand-off worth
// "a whole hand-written bracket ladder" and "24 of 24 quote-blind cells". Verified at
// THIS commit by grep, not by reading: `emit_classify.vl` defines **no**
// `shapeInnerFieldSplit` — it imports this one and calls it at exactly the six sites the
// hand-off named (`funcTypeShapeLowerable`, `variantNestedShapeOk`,
// `internNonLowerableFieldShapes`, `internShapeFieldElems`, `internInlineShape`,
// `internShapeArms`). The retirement HAPPENED; the paragraph describing it as pending
// outlived it. **A HEADER COMMENT IS NOT EVIDENCE — grep the tree before quoting a
// blocker, including one written here.**
//
// The genuinely open item that hand-off was pointing at was a DIFFERENT function:
// `emit_classify.shapeFieldParse`, which parsed the same grammar with its own
// `angOpen`/`tyGtIsClose` ladder and was the compiler's LAST hand-written type-name depth
// walk. D-SHAPEFIELD retired it onto `tyTopLevelSplit`; see its header for the
// measurement. The quote-awareness this function gained from riding the checker's home
// reached that scanner in the same move.
//
// The depth walk itself is `tyTopLevelSplit`'s, shared with every other top-level split
// in this file. Depth-aware over `{}` `[]` `()` AND the generic-application brackets: a
// field whose type is a generic APPLICATION carries the argument list's own `,`
// (`{v: Pair<i32, string>}`) and a bracket-blind depth splits the FIELD list at it. `>`
// closes only against an OPEN `<` (`tyGtIsClose`) — two other string languages ride this
// scanner and both spell a `>` that closes nothing: `tyToStr`'s `->` render and the
// `$fnsig` key's `ii>i`, and counting those runs the depth NEGATIVE. Applying that repair
// one scanner at a time is what #1118/#1120 had to do; ONE walk is why they cannot drift
// apart again.
//
// A part with no `name:` prefix is MALFORMED and pushes an EMPTY name (a well-formed
// field name always has at least one character before its `:`, so `outNames[i] == ""`
// is exactly the `ci <= 0` test each copy applied inline). The split is PURE — every
// caller's per-field side effects run in the same left-to-right order they did when
// they were interleaved with the scan.
export function shapeInnerFieldSplit(
  inner: string,
  outNames: string[],
  outTexts: string[],
) {
  const parts: string[] = []
  tyTopLevelSplit(inner, ',', false, parts)
  let k = 0
  while k < parts.length {
    const part = parts[k]
    const ci = part.indexOf(":")
    if ci <= 0 {
      outNames.push("")
      outTexts.push("")
    } else {
      outNames.push(part.slice(0, ci))
      outTexts.push(part.slice(ci + 1, part.length))
    }
    k = k + 1
  }
  0
}
// True if `name` is a bare CANONICAL STRUCT shape name — a `{…}` group that is not a map
// (`{[`), carries no TOP-LEVEL arrow, and is SPACE-FREE. The gate its three
// `nameIsStructWith*Field` consumers each spelled out identically; the space test is the
// load-bearing half — a `tyToStr` obj render (`{f: () -> i32 | boolean}`) is NOT an
// `sNames` key, and its `->` hides a closure field from `annArrowAt`, so any space marks
// a non-canonical name and rejects it.
export function canonBareShapeName(name: string) {
